var e={level:`C1`,type:`verb-form`,questions:[{id:`c1-vf-001`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Had I known about the delay, I ___ earlier.`,explanation:`Third conditional with inversion: Had + subject + past participle, ... would have + past participle.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The inversion 'Had I known' refers to the past, so we need 'would have left'.`,2:`'will have left' is a future perfect.`,3:`'had left' is not the result clause of a conditional.`},verb:`leave`,options:[`would leave`,`would have left`,`will have left`,`had left`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-002`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`it-is-time`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`It's high time we ___ working.`,explanation:`After 'it's (high) time' we use the past simple to talk about the present.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'it's high time' the verb is in the past simple.`,2:`'will' is not used after 'it's time'.`,3:`The present perfect is not used here.`},verb:`start`,options:[`start`,`started`,`will start`,`have started`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-003`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If the bridge had been inspected properly, it ___ collapse last winter.`,explanation:`Third conditional: if + past perfect, would (not) have + past participle for an unreal past. Example: If I had seen it, I would have stopped.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'won't' refers to the future, but the collapse happened last winter.`,2:`'wouldn't collapse' describes an unreal present or future, not a past event.`,3:`'hadn't' belongs in the if-clause, not in the result clause.`},verb:`collapse`,options:[`won't`,`wouldn't have`,`wouldn't`,`hadn't`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-004`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If she hadn't accepted the job in Singapore, she ___ in London now.`,explanation:`Mixed conditional: a past condition (past perfect) with a present result (would + infinitive). Example: If I had studied law, I would be a lawyer now.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'would have lived' is a past result, but 'now' shows the result is in the present.`,2:`'will live' is a real future form and cannot follow an unreal past condition.`,3:`The past perfect is used in the if-clause, not in the result clause.`},verb:`live`,options:[`would still be living`,`would still have lived`,`will still live`,`had still lived`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-005`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If I were more organised, I ___ my tax return so late last year.`,explanation:`Mixed conditional: a present general condition (past simple) with a past result (would have + past participle). Example: If he were braver, he would have spoken up.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'wouldn't submit' gives a present result, but 'last year' needs a past result.`,1:`'won't submit' is a real future form, not an unreal past result.`,2:`The past perfect alone cannot express the result of a conditional.`},verb:`submit`,options:[`wouldn't submit`,`won't submit`,`hadn't submitted`,`wouldn't have submitted`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-006`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Should you require any further information, please ___ our support team.`,explanation:`'Should you...' is a formal inverted first conditional, often followed by an imperative. Example: Should you need help, call me.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'please' an imperative uses the bare infinitive, not 'to'.`,1:`The -ing form cannot be used as an imperative after 'please'.`,3:`A past participle cannot function as an imperative.`},verb:`contact`,options:[`to contact`,`contacting`,`contact`,`contacted`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-007`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Were the government to raise taxes again, many small firms ___ out of business.`,explanation:`'Were + subject + to-infinitive' is a formal inverted second conditional; the result uses would + infinitive. Example: Were he to resign, we would need a new leader.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'will go' is a real future, but 'Were... to' signals a hypothetical situation.`,2:`The past simple is not used in the result clause of a conditional.`,3:`The past perfect does not express a hypothetical result.`},verb:`go`,options:[`will go`,`would go`,`went`,`had gone`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-008`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Unless the samples ___ at a low temperature, they will be useless for the study.`,explanation:`In first conditional clauses with 'unless', we use a present tense for future meaning. Example: Unless it is repaired, it will break.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'will' is not normally used after 'unless' to refer to the future.`,2:`The past simple suggests an unreal condition, but the main clause uses 'will'.`,3:`'would be stored' does not fit a real condition with 'will' in the main clause.`},verb:`store`,options:[`are stored`,`will be stored`,`were stored`,`would be stored`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-009`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If the referee had not stopped the match, someone ___ seriously injured.`,explanation:`Third conditional: 'might have + past participle' expresses a possible result in the unreal past. Example: If we had left later, we might have missed the train.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'might get' refers to the present or future, not a past possibility.`,1:`'may get' describes a real future possibility, not an unreal past one.`,2:`The past perfect alone does not form a conditional result.`},verb:`get`,options:[`might get`,`may get`,`had got`,`might have got`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-010`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Supposing the flight ___ cancelled, what would you do?`,explanation:`'Supposing' can introduce an imagined present or future situation with the past simple, followed by 'would'. Example: Supposing you won, what would you buy?`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present tense suggests a real situation, but the question uses 'would'.`,1:`'had been' refers to an unreal past, which does not match 'what would you do'.`,3:`'will' is not used after 'supposing' in a hypothetical question.`},verb:`be`,options:[`is`,`had been`,`were`,`will be`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-011`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Had the jury heard that evidence, the verdict ___ very different.`,explanation:`Inverted third conditional: Had + subject + past participle, with might/would have + past participle in the result. Example: Had she asked, I would have helped.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'might be' gives a present result, but the verdict was in the past.`,2:`'may be' describes a real present possibility, not an unreal past one.`,3:`The past perfect alone cannot form the result of a conditional.`},verb:`be`,options:[`might be`,`might have been`,`may be`,`had been`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-012`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If you heat ice above zero degrees, it ___.`,explanation:`Zero conditional: present simple in both clauses for general truths and scientific facts. Example: If you mix red and blue, you get purple.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'would melt' suggests an imaginary situation, but this is a scientific fact.`,2:`The future perfect does not describe a general truth.`,3:`The past simple does not express a fact that is always true.`},verb:`melt`,options:[`melts`,`would melt`,`will have melted`,`melted`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-013`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`But for the volunteers, the festival ___ place last weekend.`,explanation:`'But for' means 'if it had not been for' and introduces an unreal past condition, so the result uses could/would have + past participle. Example: But for your help, I would have failed.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'could never take' gives a present result, but the festival was last weekend.`,1:`'can never take' is a real present form, not an unreal result.`,2:`The past perfect alone does not express the result of an unreal condition.`},verb:`take`,options:[`could never take`,`can never take`,`had never taken`,`could never have taken`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-014`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If the company ___ its prices, it would attract more young customers.`,explanation:`Second conditional: if + past simple, would + infinitive for an unlikely or imaginary present/future situation. Example: If I had more time, I would travel.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple belongs to the first conditional, which uses 'will', not 'would'.`,1:`'had lowered' refers to the past, but 'would attract' points to the present or future.`,3:`'will' is not used in the if-clause of a conditional.`},verb:`lower`,options:[`lowers`,`had lowered`,`lowered`,`will lower`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-015`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Provided that all the paperwork ___ by Friday, the loan will be approved.`,explanation:`After 'provided that' we use a present tense to talk about the future. Example: Provided that it is sunny, we will go.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'will' is not used after 'provided that' for future meaning.`,2:`'would be completed' does not match 'will be approved' in the main clause.`,3:`The paperwork does not complete itself, so a passive form is needed.`},verb:`complete`,options:[`will be completed`,`is completed`,`would be completed`,`completes`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-016`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If he were really as talented as he claims, he ___ the competition years ago.`,explanation:`Mixed conditional: a present state (were) with a past result (would have + past participle). Example: If she were rich, she would have bought that house.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'would win' gives a present result, but 'years ago' needs a past result.`,2:`'will win' is a real future form, not an unreal past result.`,3:`The past perfect alone is not used in a conditional result clause.`},verb:`win`,options:[`would have won`,`would win`,`will win`,`had won`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-017`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Should the alarm ___, leave the building immediately by the nearest exit.`,explanation:`In inverted conditionals with 'should', the verb after the subject is in the bare infinitive. Example: Should it rain, we will stay inside.`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'should' the verb has no -s ending.`,1:`After 'should' we never use the past simple.`,2:`'should' is a modal and is not followed by 'to'.`},verb:`sound`,options:[`sounds`,`sounded`,`to sound`,`sound`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-018`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If it hadn't been for the satellite images, the rescuers ___ the missing climbers.`,explanation:`'If it hadn't been for' introduces an unreal past condition; the result uses would have + past participle. Example: If it hadn't been for the map, we would have got lost.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'would never find' gives a present result, but the rescue is in the past.`,1:`'will never find' is a real future form, not an unreal past result.`,3:`The past perfect alone cannot be the result of a conditional.`},verb:`find`,options:[`would never find`,`will never find`,`would never have found`,`had never found`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-019`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Even if the bill ___ next month, its effects won't be felt for years.`,explanation:`'Even if' in a real future condition is followed by a present tense. Example: Even if it is cheap, I won't buy it.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'will' is not used after 'even if' for a real future condition.`,2:`'would be passed' does not match 'won't be felt' in the main clause.`,3:`A bill is passed by someone, so a passive present form is needed, not the active past.`},verb:`pass`,options:[`will be passed`,`is passed`,`would be passed`,`passed`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-020`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If I had been told the truth from the start, I ___ so angry now.`,explanation:`Mixed conditional: past condition (past perfect) with a present result (would + infinitive). Example: If I had slept more, I wouldn't be tired now.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'wouldn't have felt' is a past result, but 'now' requires a present result.`,2:`'won't feel' is a real future form, not an unreal present result.`,3:`The past perfect is used for the condition, not for the result.`},verb:`feel`,options:[`wouldn't feel`,`wouldn't have felt`,`won't feel`,`hadn't felt`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-021`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`As long as the patient ___ the medication regularly, the symptoms should disappear.`,explanation:`'As long as' introduces a real condition and is followed by a present tense for future meaning. Example: As long as you train, you will improve.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'will' is not normally used after 'as long as'.`,1:`'would take' suggests an imaginary situation, which does not fit this real advice.`,2:`The past simple suggests an unreal situation, not a real condition.`},verb:`take`,options:[`will take`,`would take`,`took`,`takes`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-022`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Were it not for the new vaccine, the disease ___ much more widely.`,explanation:`'Were it not for' is a formal inverted second conditional meaning 'if it were not for'; the result uses would + infinitive. Example: Were it not for her, I would give up.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'will spread' is a real future, but 'Were it not for' signals an imaginary situation.`,1:`The present simple states a fact, not a hypothetical result.`,3:`The past perfect cannot be the result of a present hypothetical condition.`},verb:`spread`,options:[`will spread`,`spreads`,`would spread`,`had spread`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-023`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If the river ___ its banks again tonight, the village will have to be evacuated.`,explanation:`First conditional: if + present simple, will + infinitive for a real future possibility. Example: If it snows, the roads will close.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'will' is not used in the if-clause of a first conditional.`,2:`'would burst' belongs to the second conditional, but the main clause uses 'will'.`,3:`With a singular subject like 'the river', the present simple needs -s.`},verb:`burst`,options:[`will burst`,`bursts`,`would burst`,`burst`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-024`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If the architects had listened to local residents, the new library ___ such an eyesore today.`,explanation:`Mixed conditional: an unreal past condition with a present result, shown by 'today'. Example: If they had planned better, the road wouldn't be so busy now.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'wouldn't have been' refers to the past, but 'today' needs a present result.`,2:`'won't be' is a real future form, not an unreal present result.`,3:`The past perfect belongs to the condition, not the result.`},verb:`be`,options:[`wouldn't be`,`wouldn't have been`,`won't be`,`hadn't been`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-025`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Had it not been for her quick thinking, the fire ___ the whole warehouse.`,explanation:`'Had it not been for' is an inverted third conditional; the result uses would have + past participle. Example: Had it not been for the rain, we would have won.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'would destroy' gives a present result, but the fire happened in the past.`,1:`'will destroy' is a real future form, not an unreal past result.`,2:`The past perfect alone does not express the result of a conditional.`},verb:`destroy`,options:[`would destroy`,`will destroy`,`had destroyed`,`would have destroyed`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-026`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`I'll lend you the car on condition that you ___ it back with a full tank.`,explanation:`'On condition that' introduces a real condition and takes a present tense for future meaning. Example: You can go on condition that you call me.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'will' is not used after 'on condition that' for future reference.`,1:`'would bring' does not match the real future 'I'll lend'.`,3:`The past simple suggests an unreal situation, but this is a real promise.`},verb:`bring`,options:[`will bring`,`would bring`,`bring`,`brought`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-027`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If we ___ the warning signs earlier, the crisis could have been avoided.`,explanation:`Third conditional: if + past perfect for an unreal past condition, with could have + past participle in the result. Example: If we had left earlier, we could have caught the bus.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The past simple is used in the second conditional, but 'could have been avoided' needs a past perfect condition.`,2:`'would' is not used in the if-clause of a conditional.`,3:`The present perfect does not express an unreal past condition.`},verb:`notice`,options:[`noticed`,`had noticed`,`would notice`,`have noticed`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-028`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`The committee insisted that the report ___ before the end of the month.`,explanation:`After verbs like 'insist', 'demand' or 'recommend' + that, formal English uses the subjunctive (bare infinitive). Example: They demanded that he be dismissed.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The indicative 'is published' is not used in the formal subjunctive after 'insisted that'.`,2:`'will' is not used in the subjunctive after 'insist that'.`,3:`The report does not publish anything itself, so an active continuous form is wrong.`},verb:`publish`,options:[`be published`,`is published`,`will be published`,`was publishing`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-029`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`It is essential that every passenger ___ a valid passport.`,explanation:`After 'It is essential/vital/important that' the subjunctive uses the bare infinitive for all persons. Example: It is vital that she attend.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The subjunctive has no -s ending, even with 'every passenger'.`,1:`The past simple is not used after 'It is essential that'.`,2:`'will' is not used in the subjunctive clause.`},verb:`carry`,options:[`carries`,`carried`,`will carry`,`carry`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-030`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`I wish my neighbours ___ so much noise every night; I can't sleep.`,explanation:`'Wish + would' expresses irritation about someone else's repeated behaviour that we want to change. Example: I wish you would stop interrupting.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'didn't make' describes a present state; for annoying habits of others we use 'would'.`,1:`'hadn't made' refers to a regret about the past, not a current habit.`,3:`The present simple is not used after 'wish'.`},verb:`make`,options:[`didn't make`,`hadn't made`,`wouldn't make`,`don't make`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-031`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If only I ___ that email to the whole department yesterday!`,explanation:`'If only + past perfect' expresses regret about something in the past. Example: If only I had listened to you.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The past simple after 'if only' refers to the present, but 'yesterday' needs the past perfect.`,2:`'wouldn't send' is used for present annoyance, not past regret.`,3:`The present simple is not used after 'if only'.`},verb:`send`,options:[`didn't send`,`hadn't sent`,`wouldn't send`,`don't send`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-032`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`It's about time the council ___ something about the potholes on this road.`,explanation:`After 'It's (about/high) time' + subject, we use the past simple with present meaning. Example: It's time we left.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present simple is not used after 'It's about time + subject'.`,2:`The present perfect is not used after 'It's time'.`,3:`'would do' is not used after 'It's about time'.`},verb:`do`,options:[`did`,`does`,`has done`,`would do`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-033`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`I'd rather you ___ my phone without asking.`,explanation:`'Would rather + subject + past simple' expresses a preference about someone else's present or future action. Example: I'd rather you stayed.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple is not used after 'I'd rather + another subject'.`,1:`'not use' is not a correct verb form after 'I'd rather you'.`,2:`The past perfect would refer to a past action, which does not fit a general request.`},verb:`use`,options:[`don't use`,`not use`,`hadn't used`,`didn't use`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-034`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`She talks about the project as if she ___ it all by herself, but it was a team effort.`,explanation:`'As if + past perfect' shows that something in the past is not true. Example: He acts as if he had won the prize.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple suggests it is true and does not show the unreal past.`,1:`The present perfect does not show that the claim is untrue.`,3:`'would design' does not refer to a past action.`},verb:`design`,options:[`designs`,`has designed`,`had designed`,`would design`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-035`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`The doctor recommended that he ___ red meat for a few weeks.`,explanation:`After 'recommend that' formal English uses the subjunctive (bare infinitive). Example: She suggested that he rest.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The subjunctive does not take -s with 'he'.`,2:`The -ing form cannot be the main verb of the that-clause.`,3:`'will' is not used in the subjunctive after 'recommend'.`},verb:`avoid`,options:[`avoids`,`avoid`,`avoiding`,`will avoid`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-036`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`I wish I ___ to the conference last week; everyone says it was brilliant.`,explanation:`'Wish + past perfect' expresses regret about the past. Example: I wish I had studied harder.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The past simple after 'wish' refers to the present, but 'last week' needs the past perfect.`,2:`'wish + would' is for other people's habits, not our own past regrets.`,3:`The present perfect is not used after 'wish'.`},verb:`go`,options:[`had gone`,`went`,`would go`,`have gone`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-037`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Far from being a minor issue, it is crucial that this error ___ immediately.`,explanation:`After 'It is crucial that' formal English uses the subjunctive; in the passive this is 'be + past participle'. Example: It is vital that the door be locked.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The indicative 'is corrected' is not the formal subjunctive form.`,1:`An error does not correct itself, so the active form is wrong.`,2:`'will correct' is active and uses 'will', which is not part of the subjunctive.`},verb:`correct`,options:[`is corrected`,`corrects`,`will correct`,`be corrected`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-038`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If I ___ you, I would ask for a second opinion before signing.`,explanation:`In unreal conditions, formal English uses 'were' for all persons, including 'I'. Example: If I were rich, I would travel.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present 'am' suggests a real situation, but I cannot be you.`,1:`'had been' refers to the past, but 'would ask' points to the present.`,3:`'would' is not used in the if-clause.`},verb:`be`,options:[`am`,`had been`,`were`,`would be`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-039`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Would you rather we ___ the meeting until next week?`,explanation:`'Would rather + another subject + past simple' asks about a preference for the present or future. Example: Would you rather I drove?`,wrongReasons:{0:`After 'would rather + another subject' we use the past simple, not the present.`,2:`'will' cannot follow 'would rather + subject'.`,3:`The past perfect would refer to the past, but this is about next week.`},verb:`postpone`,options:[`postpone`,`postponed`,`will postpone`,`had postponed`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-040`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`The union demanded that the management ___ the new contracts immediately.`,explanation:`After 'demand that' formal English uses the bare infinitive (subjunctive) for every subject. Example: They demanded that he leave.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The subjunctive does not add -s, even with a singular subject like 'the management'.`,2:`A past participle alone cannot be the verb of the clause.`,3:`The -ing form cannot be the main verb of the that-clause.`},verb:`withdraw`,options:[`withdraw`,`withdraws`,`withdrawn`,`withdrawing`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-041`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`He looked at me as though I ___ mad.`,explanation:`'As though + past simple/were' describes an unreal or unlikely present situation. Example: She spoke as though she were the boss.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present tense 'am' does not show the unreal meaning after 'as though'.`,1:`The present perfect does not express an unreal comparison here.`,2:`'will be' is not used after 'as though' for an unreal comparison.`},verb:`be`,options:[`am`,`have been`,`will be`,`were`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-042`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`I wish I ___ speak Japanese; it would make my business trips much easier.`,explanation:`'Wish + could' expresses a wish about an ability we do not have now. Example: I wish I could swim.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present 'can' is not used after 'wish'.`,1:`'had could' does not exist; 'can' has no past participle.`,3:`'would can' is impossible because two modals cannot be combined.`},verb:`can`,options:[`can`,`had could`,`could`,`would can`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-043`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`It is imperative that the patient ___ alone during the first night.`,explanation:`The negative subjunctive is 'not + bare infinitive'; in the passive it is 'not be + past participle'. Example: It is vital that he not be disturbed.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'is not left' is indicative, not the formal subjunctive needed after 'imperative that'.`,2:`'will' is not used in the subjunctive after 'It is imperative that'.`,3:`The patient is not doing the leaving, so the active continuous is wrong.`},verb:`leave`,options:[`is not left`,`not be left`,`won't be left`,`isn't leaving`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-044`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`It's high time you ___ responsibility for your own mistakes.`,explanation:`'It's high time + subject + past simple' says that something should already be happening. Example: It's high time we went home.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present simple is not used after 'It's high time + subject'.`,2:`The present perfect is not used after 'It's high time'.`,3:`'would take' is not used in this structure.`},verb:`take`,options:[`took`,`take`,`have taken`,`would take`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-045`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If only the weather ___ a bit warmer, we could eat outside tonight.`,explanation:`'If only + past simple/were' expresses a wish about the present. Example: If only I were taller.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple is not used after 'if only'.`,1:`The past perfect refers to the past, but 'tonight' shows a present wish.`,2:`'will be' is not used after 'if only'.`},verb:`be`,options:[`is`,`had been`,`will be`,`were`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-046`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`The judge ordered that the defendant ___ in custody until the trial.`,explanation:`After 'order that' in formal English, the verb is in the subjunctive (bare infinitive). Example: The court ordered that she pay the fine.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The subjunctive has no -s ending.`,1:`A that-clause needs a finite or subjunctive verb, not a to-infinitive.`,3:`'will' is not used in the subjunctive.`},verb:`remain`,options:[`remains`,`to remain`,`remain`,`will remain`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-047`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`I'd sooner you ___ my parents about the accident; they'd only worry.`,explanation:`'Would sooner' works like 'would rather': + another subject + past simple for a present or future preference. Example: I'd sooner you didn't smoke here.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple is not used after 'I'd sooner + another subject'.`,2:`'not tell' is not a correct form after 'I'd sooner you'.`,3:`The past perfect refers to a past action, but this is about the future.`},verb:`tell`,options:[`don't tell`,`didn't tell`,`not tell`,`hadn't told`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-048`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Suppose he ___ the truth all along — how would we have explained our decision?`,explanation:`'Suppose + past perfect' imagines a different past situation. Example: Suppose you had missed the bus — what would you have done?`,wrongReasons:{1:`'knew' refers to an imagined present, but 'all along' points to the past.`,2:`The present simple does not express an imagined situation.`,3:`'would' is not used after 'suppose' in the condition.`},verb:`know`,options:[`had known`,`knew`,`knows`,`would know`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-049`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Many experts propose that the speed limit ___ to 30 km/h in residential areas.`,explanation:`After 'propose that' formal English uses the subjunctive; the passive is 'be + past participle'. Example: I propose that the meeting be cancelled.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The indicative 'is reduced' is not the formal subjunctive after 'propose that'.`,1:`The speed limit does not reduce itself, so an active form is wrong.`,2:`'will reduce' is active and not a subjunctive form.`},verb:`reduce`,options:[`is reduced`,`reduces`,`will reduce`,`be reduced`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-050`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`I wish you ___ me earlier; I could have helped you.`,explanation:`'Wish + past perfect' expresses regret about something that did not happen in the past. Example: I wish you had called.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The past simple after 'wish' refers to the present, but 'could have helped' shows a past regret.`,1:`'would tell' is about a present annoying habit, not a past regret.`,3:`The present perfect is not used after 'wish'.`},verb:`tell`,options:[`told`,`would tell`,`had told`,`have told`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-051`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`The chairwoman asked that all phones ___ off during the ceremony.`,explanation:`After 'ask that' formal English uses the subjunctive; in the passive this is 'be + past participle'. Example: She asked that the door be closed.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The indicative 'are switched' is not the formal subjunctive after 'asked that'.`,2:`'switched' alone is not a complete passive form.`,3:`'will' is not used in the subjunctive clause.`},verb:`switch`,options:[`are switched`,`be switched`,`switched`,`will be switched`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-052`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`subjunctive`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`She wishes she ___ more time to spend with her children these days.`,explanation:`'Wish + past simple' expresses a wish about the present. Example: He wishes he lived nearer the sea.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present simple is not used after 'wish'.`,2:`'wish + would' is not used for our own states like 'have time'.`,3:`'had had' refers to the past, but 'these days' shows the present.`},verb:`have`,options:[`had`,`has`,`would have`,`had had`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-053`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The ancient manuscript is believed ___ in a monastery in the twelfth century.`,explanation:`'Be believed/said/thought + perfect passive infinitive' reports a past action done to the subject. Example: The castle is thought to have been built in 1200.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'to be written' refers to the present or future, but the writing happened in the twelfth century.`,1:`'to have written' is active, but the manuscript did not write anything.`,2:`'being written' does not follow 'is believed'.`},verb:`write`,options:[`to be written`,`to have written`,`being written`,`to have been written`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-054`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The new hospital wing ___ by the time the minister visits next spring.`,explanation:`Future perfect passive: will have been + past participle for an action finished before a future time. Example: The report will have been sent by noon.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'will have completed' is active, but the wing does not complete itself.`,1:`The future continuous active does not fit; the wing is acted upon.`,3:`The present perfect does not refer to a point in the future.`},verb:`complete`,options:[`will have completed`,`will be completing`,`will have been completed`,`has been completed`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-055`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The suspect is reported ___ the country on a false passport last night.`,explanation:`'Is reported + perfect infinitive' refers to a past action by the subject. Example: She is said to have resigned yesterday.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'to leave' refers to the present or future, but 'last night' requires a perfect infinitive.`,2:`'to be left' is passive, but the suspect did the leaving.`,3:`'to have been left' is passive and means someone left the suspect.`},verb:`leave`,options:[`to leave`,`to have left`,`to be left`,`to have been left`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-056`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`When I arrived at the garage, my car ___, so I had to wait in the café.`,explanation:`Past continuous passive: was/were being + past participle for an action in progress done to the subject. Example: The room was being cleaned when I came in.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'was repairing' is active, but the car cannot repair itself.`,2:`The past perfect active wrongly suggests the car did the repairing.`,3:`The past simple active makes the car the doer of the action.`},verb:`repair`,options:[`was still being repaired`,`was still repairing`,`had still repaired`,`still repaired`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-057`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`It ___ that the economy will recover by the end of next year.`,explanation:`Impersonal passive: It is + past participle (said, expected, thought) + that-clause. Example: It is said that the house is haunted.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'It expects' is active, but 'it' cannot be the one doing the expecting.`,1:`The present continuous active makes 'it' the doer, which is wrong here.`,2:`'has expected' is active and does not form the impersonal passive.`},verb:`expect`,options:[`expects`,`is expecting`,`has expected`,`is expected`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-058`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`I can't stand ___ what to do by people who know less than I do.`,explanation:`After verbs like 'can't stand', 'hate' or 'enjoy' we use the passive gerund: being + past participle. Example: She hates being kept waiting.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'telling' is active and means I am the one giving instructions.`,1:`'to tell' is active and means I tell others, which is not the meaning here.`,3:`The bare infinitive cannot follow 'can't stand'.`},verb:`tell`,options:[`telling`,`to tell`,`being told`,`be told`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-059`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`By the time the firefighters arrived, most of the paintings ___.`,explanation:`Past perfect passive: had been + past participle for an action completed before another past moment. Example: The tickets had been sold before we got there.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'had destroyed' is active, but the paintings did not destroy anything.`,2:`The past continuous active makes the paintings the doers.`,3:`The present perfect does not fit a sequence of past events.`},verb:`destroy`,options:[`had already destroyed`,`had already been destroyed`,`were already destroying`,`have already been destroyed`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-060`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`She had her portrait ___ by a well-known artist for her sixtieth birthday.`,explanation:`Causative 'have something done': have + object + past participle when someone else does it for us. Example: I had my hair cut.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The bare infinitive after 'have + object' means someone performs an action, but the portrait cannot paint.`,2:`The -ing form would mean the portrait itself was painting.`,3:`'to paint' is not used in the causative 'have something done'.`},verb:`paint`,options:[`painted`,`paint`,`painting`,`to paint`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-061`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The documents need ___ before they can be submitted to the court.`,explanation:`'Need + -ing' has a passive meaning: something needs to be done. Example: The windows need cleaning.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The bare infinitive cannot follow 'need' as a main verb here.`,1:`'need to translate' is active and would mean the documents translate something.`,2:`A past participle alone cannot follow 'need'.`},verb:`translate`,options:[`translate`,`to translate`,`translated`,`translating`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-062`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The bridge ___ at the moment, so traffic is being diverted through the town centre.`,explanation:`Present continuous passive: am/is/are being + past participle for an action in progress now. Example: The road is being widened.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'is rebuilding' is active, but the bridge does not rebuild itself.`,1:`'has rebuilt' is active and not about an action in progress.`,3:`The present simple active does not describe what is happening at the moment.`},verb:`rebuild`,options:[`is rebuilding`,`has rebuilt`,`is being rebuilt`,`rebuilds`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-063`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`Several protesters are said ___ during the clashes, though the police deny this.`,explanation:`'Are said + perfect passive infinitive' reports a past event that happened to the subject. Example: Two people are thought to have been arrested.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'to have injured' is active and means the protesters hurt someone.`,2:`The continuous active infinitive changes the meaning completely.`,3:`'to injure' is active and refers to the present or future.`},verb:`injure`,options:[`to have injured`,`to have been injured`,`to be injuring`,`to injure`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-064`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The results ___ until all the data has been checked by an independent panel.`,explanation:`Future passive: will (not) be + past participle. Example: The winner will be announced tomorrow.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'won't announce' is active, but results cannot announce anything.`,2:`The present continuous active makes the results the doers.`,3:`'haven't announced' is active and does not fit a future time clause.`},verb:`announce`,options:[`won't be announced`,`won't announce`,`aren't announcing`,`haven't announced`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-065`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`Our flat was burgled while we were away, and my laptop ___.`,explanation:`'Get + past participle' is an informal passive, often for unexpected or unpleasant events. Example: His bike got damaged.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'got stealing' is not a passive form.`,1:`'stole' is active and means the laptop stole something.`,2:`'has stolen' is active and also does not fit the past narrative.`},verb:`steal`,options:[`got stealing`,`stole`,`has stolen`,`got stolen`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-066`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The minister denied ___ by the oil companies to change the law.`,explanation:`After 'deny' we use the gerund; 'having been + past participle' is the perfect passive gerund for a past action done to the subject. Example: He denied having been bribed.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'having pressured' is active and means the minister pressured someone.`,1:`'deny' is followed by a gerund, not an infinitive.`,3:`'pressuring' is active and means the minister did the pressuring.`},verb:`pressure`,options:[`having pressured`,`to be pressured`,`having been pressured`,`pressuring`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-067`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`This species of frog ___ in the wild since the 1980s.`,explanation:`Present perfect passive: has/have been + past participle, often with 'since' for a period up to now. Example: The painting hasn't been found since 1990.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'hasn't seen' is active and means the frog did not see something.`,2:`The past simple does not combine with 'since the 1980s' for a period up to now.`,3:`The present simple does not express a period from the past until now.`},verb:`see`,options:[`hasn't seen`,`hasn't been seen`,`wasn't seen`,`isn't seen`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-068`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`Applicants are required ___ two references along with their application.`,explanation:`'Be required + to-infinitive' is a passive structure; the infinitive itself is active when the subject does the action. Example: Students are required to wear uniforms.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'to be provided' would mean the applicants are provided, which makes no sense.`,2:`'be required' is followed by a to-infinitive, not a gerund.`,3:`The perfect passive infinitive wrongly makes the applicants the thing provided.`},verb:`provide`,options:[`to provide`,`to be provided`,`providing`,`to have been provided`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-069`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The film is thought ___ in a remote part of Iceland at the moment.`,explanation:`'Be thought + continuous passive infinitive' (to be being + past participle) describes an action in progress now done to the subject. Example: The bridge is said to be being repaired.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'to have shot' is active and past, but the filming is happening now.`,1:`'to shoot' is active and means the film does the shooting.`,2:`'to be shooting' is active; the film cannot shoot anything.`},verb:`shoot`,options:[`to have shot`,`to shoot`,`to be shooting`,`to be being shot`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-070`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`I'm not used to ___ so much attention; it makes me feel uncomfortable.`,explanation:`After 'be used to' the preposition 'to' is followed by a gerund; the passive gerund is being + past participle. Example: He isn't used to being criticised.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'paying' is active and means I give attention to others.`,1:`After the preposition 'to' we need a gerund, not a bare infinitive.`,3:`The bare infinitive cannot follow the preposition 'to' in 'be used to'.`},verb:`pay`,options:[`paying`,`be paid`,`being paid`,`pay`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-071`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`Visitors are asked not to touch the sculptures, which ___ from fragile materials.`,explanation:`Present simple passive: am/is/are + past participle for facts about how things are produced. Example: Paper is made from wood.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'make' is active and means the sculptures produce something.`,2:`The present continuous active makes the sculptures the doers.`,3:`'have made' is active and changes the meaning.`},verb:`make`,options:[`make`,`are made`,`are making`,`have made`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-072`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The thief must ___ through the kitchen window, as it was left open.`,explanation:`Modal + perfect infinitive (must have + past participle) is a deduction about the past. Example: She must have forgotten.`,wrongReasons:{1:`Intransitive phrasal verbs like 'get in' have no passive form.`,2:`'must be getting in' is about now, but the break-in happened in the past.`,3:`'must' is followed by an infinitive, not a past perfect form.`},verb:`get in`,options:[`have got in`,`have been got in`,`be getting in`,`had got in`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-073`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`It's no use complaining; the decision ___ and can't be reversed.`,explanation:`Present perfect passive: has been + past participle for a completed action with present results. Example: The order has been placed.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'has made' is active, but the decision did not make anything.`,1:`The present continuous active makes 'the decision' the doer.`,2:`'made' alone is active past or a participle, not a complete passive verb here.`},verb:`make`,options:[`has made`,`is making`,`made`,`has been made`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-074`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The children were made ___ their room before they were allowed to go out.`,explanation:`In the passive, 'make someone do' becomes 'be made to do' with a to-infinitive. Example: He was made to apologise.`,wrongReasons:{0:`In the passive form of 'make', the bare infinitive becomes a to-infinitive.`,1:`'were made' is not followed by the -ing form.`,3:`A past participle does not follow 'were made' in this structure.`},verb:`tidy`,options:[`tidy`,`tidying`,`to tidy`,`tidied`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-075`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`Hundreds of rare books are known ___ in the library fire of 1904.`,explanation:`'Be known + perfect passive infinitive' reports a past action done to the subject. Example: The ship is known to have been sunk in 1942.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'to be lost' refers to the present, but the fire was in 1904.`,2:`'to have lost' is active and means the books lost something.`,3:`'are known' is not followed by the -ing form here.`},verb:`lose`,options:[`to be lost`,`to have been lost`,`to have lost`,`losing`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-076`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The ceremony ___ outdoors if the weather had been better.`,explanation:`Third conditional passive: would have been + past participle. Example: The game would have been cancelled if it had rained.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'would have held' is active, but the ceremony did not hold anything.`,2:`The continuous active makes the ceremony the doer and is not past.`,3:`The past perfect belongs to the if-clause, not the result.`},verb:`hold`,options:[`would have been held`,`would have held`,`would be holding`,`had been held`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-077`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`Nobody likes ___ in front of their colleagues.`,explanation:`After 'like', 'hate' or 'enjoy', the passive gerund is being + past participle. Example: Nobody enjoys being ignored.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'criticising' is active and means people criticise others.`,1:`A past participle alone cannot follow 'like'.`,2:`The bare infinitive does not follow 'like'.`},verb:`criticise`,options:[`criticising`,`criticised`,`be criticised`,`being criticised`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-078`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ the report three times, she finally sent it to her manager.`,explanation:`A perfect participle clause (having + past participle) shows an action completed before the main action. Example: Having eaten, we left.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Checked' alone is passive in meaning and would suggest she was checked.`,1:`'Being checked' is passive and means someone was checking her.`,3:`'Having been checked' is passive and would mean she herself had been checked.`},verb:`check`,options:[`Checked`,`Being checked`,`Having checked`,`Having been checked`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-079`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ in 1890, the theatre is one of the oldest buildings in the city.`,explanation:`A past participle clause has a passive meaning and refers to the subject of the main clause. Example: Written in French, the book was hard to read.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Building' is active and would mean the theatre built something.`,2:`'Having built' is active and means the theatre did the building.`,3:`A to-infinitive here would express purpose, which makes no sense.`},verb:`build`,options:[`Building`,`Built`,`Having built`,`To build`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-080`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Anyone ___ to attend the workshop should register by Monday.`,explanation:`A present participle can replace a relative clause with an active verb: 'anyone wishing' = 'anyone who wishes'. Example: People living nearby were evacuated.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'wished' would be passive, meaning someone wished for the people.`,2:`The perfect participle refers to a completed action, but this is a current intention.`,3:`A to-infinitive after 'anyone' does not express 'who wishes' here.`},verb:`wish`,options:[`wishing`,`wished`,`having wished`,`to wish`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-081`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ by the noise, the baby started crying.`,explanation:`A past participle clause has a passive meaning: 'Woken by the noise' = 'Because she was woken by the noise'. Example: Shocked by the news, he sat down.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Waking' is active and does not fit with 'by the noise'.`,1:`'Having woken' is active and means the baby woke someone.`,2:`'Woke' is a past simple form, not a participle.`},verb:`wake`,options:[`Waking`,`Having woken`,`Woke`,`Woken`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-082`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Not ___ what else to do, he called the emergency services.`,explanation:`A negative participle clause puts 'not' before the participle and often gives a reason. Example: Not having a car, she took the bus.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'known' is passive and does not make sense with 'what else to do'.`,1:`The perfect passive participle wrongly suggests someone knew him.`,3:`'Not to know' expresses purpose or obligation, not a reason.`},verb:`know`,options:[`known`,`having been known`,`knowing`,`to know`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-083`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`The protesters, ___ banners and singing, marched towards parliament.`,explanation:`A present participle clause describes an action happening at the same time as the main verb. Example: She walked in, smiling.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'carried' would be passive, meaning someone carried the protesters.`,2:`The perfect passive form means the protesters had been carried.`,3:`A to-infinitive here would suggest purpose, which does not fit.`},verb:`carry`,options:[`carried`,`carrying`,`having been carried`,`to carry`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-084`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Most of the goods ___ in this factory are exported to Asia.`,explanation:`A reduced relative clause with a past participle has a passive meaning: 'goods produced' = 'goods which are produced'. Example: The cars made here are expensive.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'producing' is active and means the goods produce something.`,2:`'having produced' is active and refers to the goods doing the producing.`,3:`The bare form 'produce' cannot be used as a reduced relative clause.`},verb:`produce`,options:[`produced`,`producing`,`having produced`,`produce`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-085`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ been warned about the strike, we decided to travel by car.`,explanation:`'Having been + past participle' is a perfect passive participle clause, showing an earlier action done to the subject. Example: Having been told the news, she cried.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Had been warned' at the start would need inversion and a conditional meaning.`,1:`The bare form 'Have' cannot begin a participle clause.`,2:`'Has' is a finite verb and cannot start this clause.`},verb:`have`,options:[`Had`,`Have`,`Has`,`Having`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-086`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ the right to remain silent, the suspect refused to answer any questions.`,explanation:`A past participle clause has a passive meaning: 'Given the right' = 'Because he had been given the right'. Example: Offered a seat, she sat down.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Giving' is active and means the suspect gave the right to someone.`,1:`'Having given' is active and means the suspect had given something.`,3:`'Gave' is a past simple form, not a participle.`},verb:`give`,options:[`Giving`,`Having given`,`Given`,`Gave`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-087`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`There were dozens of people ___ outside the shop before it opened.`,explanation:`After 'There is/are + noun', a present participle describes what the people are doing. Example: There was a man waiting at the door.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'queued' is not used as a participle with this meaning.`,2:`The perfect participle refers to a completed action, but they were still waiting.`,3:`A to-infinitive would suggest purpose or obligation, not an ongoing action.`},verb:`queue`,options:[`queued`,`queuing`,`having queued`,`to queue`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-088`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ from a distance, the island looks like a sleeping giant.`,explanation:`A past participle clause has a passive meaning: 'Seen from a distance' = 'When it is seen from a distance'. Example: Viewed from above, the city is beautiful.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'Seeing' is active and means the island sees something.`,2:`'Having seen' is active and means the island had seen something.`,3:`'Saw' is past simple, not a participle.`},verb:`see`,options:[`Seen`,`Seeing`,`Having seen`,`Saw`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-089`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ his degree, he spent a year travelling around South America.`,explanation:`A perfect participle clause shows that one action finished before the next. Example: Having saved enough money, she bought a flat.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Finished' would be passive, meaning he was finished.`,1:`The perfect passive form means he himself had been finished.`,2:`'Being finished' is passive and does not fit 'his degree' as an object.`},verb:`finish`,options:[`Finished`,`Having been finished`,`Being finished`,`Having finished`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-090`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`The letter, ___ in a hurry, was full of spelling mistakes.`,explanation:`A past participle clause can add information about a noun with a passive meaning. Example: The cake, baked by my aunt, was delicious.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'writing' is active and means the letter was writing something.`,1:`'having written' is active and means the letter wrote something.`,3:`'wrote' is past simple, not a participle.`},verb:`write`,options:[`writing`,`having written`,`written`,`wrote`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-091`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ on a hill above the bay, the hotel offers stunning views of the sea.`,explanation:`A past participle clause with a passive meaning can describe where something is: 'Situated on a hill' = 'Since it is situated on a hill'. Example: Located near the station, the flat is very convenient.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Situating' is active and would mean the hotel places something.`,2:`'Having situated' is active and means the hotel had placed something.`,3:`The bare form cannot begin a participle clause.`},verb:`situate`,options:[`Situating`,`Situated`,`Having situated`,`Situate`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-092`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`The man ___ next to me on the plane turned out to be a famous chef.`,explanation:`A present participle can replace a relative clause: 'the man sitting' = 'the man who was sitting'. Example: The girl talking to Tom is my sister.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'sits' is a finite verb and would need 'who' before it.`,2:`The perfect participle refers to a finished action, but he was still sitting.`,3:`A to-infinitive after 'the man' does not describe what he was doing.`},verb:`sit`,options:[`sitting`,`sits`,`having sat`,`to sit`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-093`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ to the public in 2015, the museum has already welcomed over a million visitors.`,explanation:`A past participle clause with a passive meaning gives background information: 'Opened in 2015' = 'It was opened in 2015'. Example: Founded in 1900, the club is very old.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Opening' is active and suggests an action happening at the same time.`,1:`'Being opened' suggests the process is still in progress.`,2:`A to-infinitive would express purpose, which makes no sense here.`},verb:`open`,options:[`Opening`,`Being opened`,`To open`,`Opened`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-094`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ the risks involved, I still think the plan is worth trying.`,explanation:`'Considering' can work like a preposition meaning 'taking into account'. Example: Considering his age, he runs very fast.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Considered' would make the risks or the speaker the thing being considered.`,1:`The perfect passive wrongly suggests that I was considered.`,3:`'To be considered' expresses obligation, not 'taking into account'.`},verb:`consider`,options:[`Considered`,`Having been considered`,`Considering`,`To be considered`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-095`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Once ___, the instructions are surprisingly easy to follow.`,explanation:`Conjunctions like 'once', 'when' or 'if' can be followed by a past participle with a passive meaning. Example: Once opened, eat within three days.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'understanding' is active and means the instructions understand something.`,2:`The bare form cannot follow 'once' in a reduced clause.`,3:`'having understood' is active and makes the instructions the doer.`},verb:`understand`,options:[`understanding`,`understood`,`understand`,`having understood`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-096`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`The weather ___ fine, we decided to have lunch in the garden.`,explanation:`An absolute participle clause has its own subject before the participle: 'The weather being fine' = 'As the weather was fine'. Example: The work done, we went home.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'been' is a past participle and cannot be used alone after a subject here.`,2:`'having' does not make sense with 'fine'.`,3:`The bare form 'be' cannot follow a subject without a modal.`},verb:`be`,options:[`being`,`been`,`having`,`be`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-097`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Anyone ___ smoking on the train will be fined.`,explanation:`A reduced relative clause with a past participle has a passive meaning: 'anyone caught' = 'anyone who is caught'. Example: Students found cheating will fail.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'catching' is active and means the person catches someone else.`,1:`'having caught' is active and changes the meaning.`,2:`A to-infinitive does not give the meaning 'who is caught'.`},verb:`catch`,options:[`catching`,`having caught`,`to catch`,`caught`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-098`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ at the airport, we were met by our guide and taken straight to the hotel.`,explanation:`'On + -ing' means 'as soon as' or 'when'. Example: On hearing the news, she called her mother.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'arrive' is intransitive, so 'Arrived' cannot be used as a participle clause here.`,1:`'Being arrived' is not possible because 'arrive' has no passive.`,3:`A to-infinitive would express purpose, not time.`},verb:`arrive`,options:[`Arrived`,`Being arrived`,`On arriving`,`To arrive`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-099`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`The money ___ at the charity concert will go to local schools.`,explanation:`A past participle after a noun has a passive meaning: 'the money raised' = 'the money that was raised'. Example: The food served was excellent.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'raising' is active and means the money raises something.`,2:`'rising' comes from 'rise', which is intransitive and is a different verb.`,3:`'having raised' is active and makes the money the doer.`},verb:`raise`,options:[`raising`,`raised`,`rising`,`having raised`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-100`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Never having ___ abroad before, she was nervous about the trip.`,explanation:`In a negative perfect participle clause, 'never/not' comes before 'having + past participle'. Example: Never having met him, I didn't know what to expect.`,wrongReasons:{1:`After 'having' we need a past participle, not the -ing form.`,2:`After 'having' we need a past participle, not the bare form.`,3:`'travel' is intransitive here, so a passive form is impossible.`},verb:`travel`,options:[`travelled`,`travelling`,`travel`,`been travelled`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-101`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`When ___ about the scandal, the actor refused to comment.`,explanation:`'When + past participle' is a reduced passive clause: 'When asked' = 'When he was asked'. Example: When questioned, she said nothing.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'asking' is active and means the actor asked someone else.`,1:`'having asked' is active and means the actor had asked.`,2:`The bare form 'ask' cannot follow 'when' in a reduced clause.`},verb:`ask`,options:[`asking`,`having asked`,`ask`,`asked`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-102`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`participle-clauses`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`___ to reach the summit before dark, the climbers set off at four in the morning.`,explanation:`A past participle used like an adjective can open a clause describing the subject's state. Example: Exhausted, they went to bed.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'Determining' means calculating or deciding something, which does not fit here.`,1:`'Having determined' means they had calculated something, not that they felt determined.`,3:`A to-infinitive would express purpose, which does not fit.`},verb:`determine`,options:[`Determining`,`Having determined`,`Determined`,`To determine`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-103`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Never before ___ such a large crowd gathered in the old square.`,explanation:`After negative adverbials like 'never before' at the start of a sentence, we invert the subject and auxiliary. Example: Never before had she felt so happy.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Without inversion the sentence is wrong after 'Never before' at the start.`,2:`The past simple without an auxiliary cannot be inverted after 'Never before'.`,3:`After 'did' the main verb must be in the base form, not the past.`},verb:`see`,options:[`I had seen`,`had I seen`,`I saw`,`did I saw`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-104`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`No sooner ___ the house than it started to pour with rain.`,explanation:`'No sooner + had + subject + past participle ... than' describes one action immediately after another. Example: No sooner had I sat down than the phone rang.`,wrongReasons:{1:`Without inversion the structure 'No sooner ... than' is incorrect.`,2:`The present perfect does not match the past tense 'started'.`,3:`The past simple without inversion is wrong after 'No sooner'.`},verb:`leave`,options:[`had we left`,`we had left`,`have we left`,`we left`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-105`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Not only ___ the exam, but she also got the highest mark in the school.`,explanation:`'Not only' at the start of a clause is followed by inversion (auxiliary + subject). Example: Not only did he lie, but he also stole.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Without inversion the sentence is wrong after a fronted 'Not only'.`,1:`'she did pass' keeps normal word order, so there is no inversion.`,2:`Modern English does not invert the subject with a main verb like 'passed'.`},verb:`pass`,options:[`she passed`,`she did pass`,`passed she`,`did she pass`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-106`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Rarely ___ a politician admit a mistake so openly.`,explanation:`Fronted 'rarely', 'seldom' or 'hardly ever' require inversion with an auxiliary. Example: Seldom do they eat out.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Without inversion the fronted 'Rarely' is ungrammatical.`,1:`'we do hear' has normal word order, so there is no inversion.`,3:`A main verb cannot be inverted with the subject in modern English.`},verb:`hear`,options:[`we hear`,`we do hear`,`do we hear`,`hear we`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-107`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Hardly ___ to speak when the microphone stopped working.`,explanation:`'Hardly + had + subject + past participle ... when' describes one event immediately followed by another. Example: Hardly had we arrived when it snowed.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Without inversion the structure 'Hardly ... when' is wrong.`,2:`The present perfect does not fit a story told with the past tense 'stopped'.`,3:`The past simple without inversion is wrong after 'Hardly'.`},verb:`begin`,options:[`he had begun`,`had he begun`,`has he begun`,`he began`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-108`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Only after the results were published ___ how serious the problem was.`,explanation:`'Only after/when/then...' at the start causes inversion in the main clause. Example: Only then did I understand.`,wrongReasons:{1:`Without inversion the main clause is wrong after 'Only after...'.`,2:`'the public did realise' has normal word order, so it is not inverted.`,3:`A main verb cannot come before the subject in this structure.`},verb:`realise`,options:[`did the public realise`,`the public realised`,`the public did realise`,`realised the public`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-109`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Under no circumstances ___ to leave the building during the test.`,explanation:`'Under no circumstances' at the start requires inversion: auxiliary + subject + main verb. Example: Under no circumstances should you open the door.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Without inversion the sentence is wrong after 'Under no circumstances'.`,1:`Only the auxiliary goes before the subject, not the whole verb.`,2:`'candidates allowed' has no auxiliary verb and is incomplete.`},verb:`allow`,options:[`candidates are allowed`,`are allowed candidates`,`candidates allowed`,`are candidates allowed`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-110`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Little ___ that her phone was being monitored by the police.`,explanation:`Fronted 'little' (meaning 'not at all') requires inversion with 'did' + base form. Example: Little did they suspect the truth.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Without inversion the sentence is wrong after a fronted 'Little'.`,1:`'she did know' keeps normal order and does not invert.`,3:`A main verb cannot be inverted with the subject in modern English.`},verb:`know`,options:[`she knew`,`she did know`,`did she know`,`knew she`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-111`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Seldom ___ such dedication in someone so young.`,explanation:`After fronted 'seldom' the auxiliary comes before the subject. Example: Seldom have I been so surprised.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Without inversion the sentence is wrong after 'Seldom'.`,2:`The past simple without inversion is incorrect after 'Seldom'.`,3:`The main verb cannot be placed before the subject.`},verb:`encounter`,options:[`I have encountered`,`have I encountered`,`I encountered`,`encountered I`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-112`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Not until the last guest had gone ___ to relax.`,explanation:`'Not until...' at the start of a sentence causes inversion in the main clause. Example: Not until midnight did he come home.`,wrongReasons:{1:`Without inversion the main clause is wrong after 'Not until...'.`,2:`'the hosts did begin' has normal order and is not inverted.`,3:`A main verb cannot be inverted with the subject.`},verb:`begin`,options:[`did the hosts begin`,`the hosts began`,`the hosts did begin`,`began the hosts`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-113`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`So complex ___ the instructions that nobody could assemble the furniture.`,explanation:`'So + adjective + be + subject + that' is an emphatic inversion; the verb agrees with the subject that follows. Example: So strong was the wind that trees fell.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'was' does not agree with the plural subject 'the instructions'.`,1:`The present tense does not match the past 'could assemble'.`,2:`'been' is a participle and cannot be the main verb.`},verb:`be`,options:[`was`,`are`,`been`,`were`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-114`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`On no account ___ the patient's records be shared with third parties.`,explanation:`'On no account' is already negative, so it is followed by an inverted positive auxiliary. Example: On no account should you lie.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'must not' adds a second negative, which reverses the meaning.`,1:`'mustn't' creates a double negative with 'On no account'.`,3:`'must' is a modal and is never followed by 'to'.`},verb:`must`,options:[`must not`,`mustn't`,`must`,`must to`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-115`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Only by working together ___ this crisis.`,explanation:`'Only by + -ing' at the start requires inversion in the main clause. Example: Only by training hard can you win.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Without inversion the main clause is wrong after 'Only by...'.`,2:`The present simple without inversion is wrong after 'Only by...'.`,3:`A main verb cannot come before the subject.`},verb:`overcome`,options:[`we can overcome`,`can we overcome`,`we overcome`,`overcome we`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-116`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Such ___ the demand for tickets that the website crashed within minutes.`,explanation:`'Such + be + subject + that' is an emphatic inversion; the verb agrees with the subject. Example: Such was his anger that he shouted.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'were' does not agree with the singular subject 'the demand'.`,2:`The present tense does not match the past 'crashed'.`,3:`'being' cannot be the main verb of the clause.`},verb:`be`,options:[`was`,`were`,`is`,`being`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-117`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Nowhere else in Europe ___ such a variety of birds in one place.`,explanation:`Fronted 'nowhere' requires inversion: auxiliary + subject + main verb. Example: Nowhere have I seen such beauty.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Without inversion the sentence is wrong after 'Nowhere else'.`,1:`The present simple without inversion is wrong after 'Nowhere'.`,2:`A main verb cannot be inverted with the subject.`},verb:`find`,options:[`you will find`,`you find`,`find you`,`will you find`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-118`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Scarcely ___ the contract when the company went bankrupt.`,explanation:`'Scarcely + had + subject + past participle ... when' shows that one thing happened right after another. Example: Scarcely had we left when it rained.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Without inversion the structure 'Scarcely ... when' is incorrect.`,1:`The present perfect cannot be combined with the past event 'went bankrupt'.`,3:`The past simple without inversion is wrong after 'Scarcely'.`},verb:`sign`,options:[`they had signed`,`have they signed`,`had they signed`,`they signed`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-119`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`At no time ___ that the project would be cancelled.`,explanation:`'At no time' at the start requires inversion; in the passive the auxiliary 'was' comes before the subject. Example: At no time was he informed.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Without inversion the sentence is wrong after 'At no time'.`,2:`After 'did' the verb must be in the base form, and here a passive is needed.`,3:`A main verb cannot be inverted with the subject.`},verb:`suggest`,options:[`it was suggested`,`was it suggested`,`did it suggested`,`suggested it`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-120`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Only when the lights went out ___ how late it was.`,explanation:`In sentences starting with 'Only when', the inversion is in the main clause, not in the when-clause. Example: Only when he left did I relax.`,wrongReasons:{1:`Without inversion the main clause is wrong after 'Only when...'.`,2:`'we did notice' keeps normal word order, so there is no inversion.`,3:`Modern English does not put a main verb before the subject.`},verb:`notice`,options:[`did we notice`,`we noticed`,`we did notice`,`noticed we`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-121`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`In no way ___ responsible for the damage caused by the storm.`,explanation:`'In no way' at the start requires inversion; with 'be' the verb itself moves before the subject. Example: In no way was it my fault.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Without inversion the sentence is wrong after 'In no way'.`,1:`'be' does not use 'does' to form inversion.`,2:`'the landlord be' has no correct finite verb.`},verb:`be`,options:[`the landlord is`,`does the landlord be`,`the landlord be`,`is the landlord`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-122`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Not once ___ for help during the whole project.`,explanation:`'Not once' at the start of a sentence requires inversion with 'did' + base form. Example: Not once did she complain.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Without inversion the sentence is wrong after 'Not once'.`,1:`'he did ask' has normal order and is not inverted.`,3:`A main verb cannot come before the subject.`},verb:`ask`,options:[`he asked`,`he did ask`,`did he ask`,`asked he`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-123`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Only later ___ that the painting was a forgery.`,explanation:`'Only later' at the start causes inversion; in the passive the auxiliary comes before the subject. Example: Only later was the truth revealed.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Without inversion the sentence is wrong after 'Only later'.`,2:`After 'did' the verb must be in the base form, and a passive is needed here.`,3:`A main verb cannot be inverted with the subject.`},verb:`discover`,options:[`it was discovered`,`was it discovered`,`did it discovered`,`discovered it`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-124`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Little ___ that their decision would change the history of the company.`,explanation:`Fronted 'Little' meaning 'not at all' is followed by 'did' + subject + base form. Example: Little did I expect a reply.`,wrongReasons:{1:`Without inversion the sentence is wrong after a fronted 'Little'.`,2:`'the founders did imagine' keeps normal order.`,3:`A main verb cannot be inverted with the subject.`},verb:`imagine`,options:[`did the founders imagine`,`the founders imagined`,`the founders did imagine`,`imagined the founders`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-125`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Not only ___ late, but he also forgot to bring the documents.`,explanation:`Fronted 'Not only' takes inversion with an auxiliary, while 'but also' keeps normal word order. Example: Not only is she smart, but she is also kind.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Without inversion the clause is wrong after a fronted 'Not only'.`,1:`'he did arrive' has normal word order and is not inverted.`,2:`A main verb cannot come before the subject.`},verb:`arrive`,options:[`he arrived`,`he did arrive`,`arrived he`,`did he arrive`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-126`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`inversion`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`Never ___ so humiliated in my entire life as I did at that dinner.`,explanation:`Fronted 'Never' requires inversion with an auxiliary; the present perfect is used for experience up to now. Example: Never have I seen such rain.`,wrongReasons:{0:`Without inversion the sentence is wrong after a fronted 'Never'.`,1:`The past simple without inversion is wrong after 'Never'.`,3:`A main verb cannot come before the subject.`},verb:`feel`,options:[`I have felt`,`I felt`,`have I felt`,`felt I`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-127`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`It was my grandmother who first ___ me how to bake bread.`,explanation:`In an 'It was ... who' cleft sentence, the verb after 'who' follows the normal tense of the event. Example: It was Anna who found the keys.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple does not match the past 'It was'.`,2:`The present perfect does not fit a finished past event introduced by 'It was'.`,3:`The bare form cannot be the verb after 'who' with a third-person subject.`},verb:`teach`,options:[`teaches`,`taught`,`has taught`,`teach`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-128`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`What I really need right now ___ a long holiday.`,explanation:`In a 'What' cleft sentence, the what-clause is singular, so the verb is 'is'. Example: What we want is peace.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The what-clause acts as a singular subject, so 'are' is wrong.`,2:`'be' alone is not a finite verb.`,3:`'were' is plural and past, but the sentence is about now.`},verb:`be`,options:[`is`,`are`,`be`,`were`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-129`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`All he did was ___ about the weather for an hour.`,explanation:`In 'All/What + subject + did + was', the verb after 'was' is usually the bare infinitive. Example: All she did was cry.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The past tense is already shown by 'did', so 'complained' is wrong after 'was'.`,1:`The -s form cannot follow 'was'.`,2:`The present perfect cannot follow 'was' in this structure.`},verb:`complain`,options:[`complained`,`complains`,`has complained`,`complain`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-130`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`It is the students, not the teachers, who ___ the most from the new timetable.`,explanation:`In 'It is ... who', the verb after 'who' agrees with the noun it refers to (here 'the students', plural). Example: It's the children who decide.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'benefits' is singular, but it refers to 'the students'.`,1:`'is benefiting' is singular and does not agree with 'the students'.`,3:`'has benefited' is singular, but the subject is plural.`},verb:`benefit`,options:[`benefits`,`is benefiting`,`benefit`,`has benefited`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-131`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`What surprised me most ___ how calm everyone remained during the crisis.`,explanation:`In a 'What' cleft, the verb is singular and follows the tense of the sentence. Example: What annoyed me was the noise.`,wrongReasons:{0:`A what-clause is singular, so 'were' is wrong.`,2:`The continuous form is not used for this kind of linking verb.`,3:`'been' is a participle and cannot be the main verb.`},verb:`be`,options:[`were`,`was`,`is being`,`been`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-132`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`It was only when the doctor called that I ___ how ill my father was.`,explanation:`In 'It was only when ... that', the main event follows in the past simple. Example: It was only when I saw her that I remembered.`,wrongReasons:{1:`The present simple does not match the past context.`,2:`The past perfect would place the realisation before the call, which is illogical.`,3:`'realise' is a state verb and is not normally used in the continuous.`},verb:`realise`,options:[`realised`,`realise`,`had realised`,`was realising`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-133`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`It's you who ___ to apologise, not me.`,explanation:`In 'It's ... who', the verb agrees with the pronoun it refers to: 'you need'. Example: It's you who are wrong.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'needs' agrees with 'it', but the verb must agree with 'you'.`,1:`The -ing form cannot be the main verb of the who-clause.`,2:`A to-infinitive cannot be the main verb of the who-clause.`},verb:`need`,options:[`needs`,`needing`,`to need`,`need`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-134`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`What the company should do ___ more in staff training.`,explanation:`In 'What + subject + do/does/should do + is', the verb after 'is' is the bare infinitive (or to-infinitive). Example: What you should do is call her.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'is invested' is passive and changes the meaning completely.`,1:`'are' does not agree with the singular what-clause.`,3:`After 'What ... do is' we do not use the -ing form.`},verb:`invest`,options:[`is invested`,`are investing`,`is invest`,`is investing`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-135`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`It was in this very room that the treaty ___ in 1919.`,explanation:`In 'It was ... that', the that-clause has a normal verb; here a past passive is needed. Example: It was here that the film was made.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'signed' is active, but the treaty did not sign anything.`,2:`The present perfect does not match the date 1919.`,3:`The present simple passive does not match a past event.`},verb:`sign`,options:[`signed`,`was signed`,`has been signed`,`is signed`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-136`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`The reason I left early ___ that I had a terrible headache.`,explanation:`In a cleft with 'The reason ... was that', the verb agrees with 'reason' and follows the past context. Example: The reason he called was that he needed help.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'were' does not agree with the singular noun 'reason'.`,2:`The continuous form is not used here.`,3:`'be' alone is not a finite verb.`},verb:`be`,options:[`was`,`were`,`is being`,`be`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-137`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`It's the noise from the motorway that ___ me awake every night.`,explanation:`In 'It's ... that', the verb in the that-clause agrees with the focused noun ('the noise' = singular). Example: It's the heat that bothers me.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'keep' is plural, but 'the noise' is singular.`,1:`The past simple does not fit 'every night' with 'It's'.`,2:`The -ing form cannot be the main verb of the clause.`},verb:`keep`,options:[`keep`,`kept`,`keeping`,`keeps`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-138`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`What we did next ___ the whole team together for an emergency meeting.`,explanation:`In 'What + subject + did + was', the bare infinitive follows 'was'. Example: What I did was phone the police.`,wrongReasons:{0:`'was called' is passive and changes the meaning.`,1:`'were' does not agree with the singular what-clause.`,3:`The -s form cannot follow 'was'.`},verb:`call`,options:[`was called`,`were calling`,`was call`,`was calls`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-139`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`It wasn't until I moved abroad that I ___ to appreciate my home town.`,explanation:`In 'It wasn't until ... that', the that-clause uses the past simple for the later event. Example: It wasn't until he left that I missed him.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple does not match the past context.`,2:`The past perfect would place it before the move, which is illogical.`,3:`The present perfect does not match 'It wasn't until'.`},verb:`begin`,options:[`begin`,`began`,`had begun`,`have begun`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-140`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`It is the parents who ___ for the children's safety on the school trip.`,explanation:`In 'It is ... who', the verb agrees with the focused noun; here a plural passive is needed. Example: It is the drivers who are blamed.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'is held' is singular, but it refers to 'the parents'.`,2:`The active form needs an object; the parents are the ones held responsible.`,3:`The active continuous has the wrong meaning; the parents are not holding anyone.`},verb:`hold`,options:[`are held responsible`,`is held responsible`,`hold responsible`,`are holding responsible`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-141`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`What she has always wanted ___ to open her own restaurant.`,explanation:`In a 'What' cleft, the what-clause is singular and takes 'is' before a to-infinitive. Example: What I want is to sleep.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The what-clause is singular, so 'are' is wrong.`,1:`'being' cannot be the main verb of the sentence.`,2:`'been' is a participle and cannot be the main verb.`},verb:`be`,options:[`are`,`being`,`been`,`is`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-142`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`It was Marie Curie who ___ the first woman to win a Nobel Prize.`,explanation:`In an 'It was ... who' cleft about a historical fact, the past simple is used. Example: It was Bell who invented the telephone.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present simple does not match a historical fact introduced by 'It was'.`,1:`The present perfect does not fit a finished historical event.`,3:`The past perfect needs another past event to come before.`},verb:`become`,options:[`becomes`,`has become`,`became`,`had become`],correct:2},{id:`c1-vf-143`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`All I want ___ for everyone to stop arguing.`,explanation:`'All I want is...' is a cleft; the all-clause is singular, so the verb is 'is'. Example: All we need is time.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The all-clause is singular, so 'are' is wrong.`,2:`'be' alone is not a finite verb.`,3:`'being' cannot be the main verb of the sentence.`},verb:`be`,options:[`are`,`is`,`be`,`being`],correct:1},{id:`c1-vf-144`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`It's not the money that ___ me; it's the lack of respect.`,explanation:`In 'It's ... that', the verb agrees with the focused noun; 'the money' is singular and the time is present. Example: It's the waiting that annoys me.`,wrongReasons:{1:`'bother' does not agree with the singular noun 'the money'.`,2:`The -ing form cannot be the main verb.`,3:`The past tense does not match the present 'it's the lack of respect'.`},verb:`bother`,options:[`bothers`,`bother`,`bothering`,`bothered`],correct:0},{id:`c1-vf-145`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`cleft-sentences`,topics:[`gerunds-infinitives-linking`],prompt:`What happened was that the server ___ during the night and all the data was lost.`,explanation:`'What happened was that...' introduces a past event, usually in the past simple. Example: What happened was that he missed the bus.`,wrongReasons:{0:`The present perfect does not match the past 'happened'.`,1:`The present simple does not match the past context.`,2:`'crash' here is intransitive, so the passive is wrong.`},verb:`crash`,options:[`has crashed`,`crashes`,`was crashed`,`crashed`],correct:3},{id:`c1-vf-151`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The scheme has been criticised ___ the grounds that it benefits only the wealthy.`,explanation:`"On the grounds that" introduces a reason.`,options:[`by`,`at`,`for`,`on`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Criticised at the grounds" is not standard.`,0:`"By the grounds" is not standard.`,2:`"For the grounds" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-vf-152`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`She was seen ___ the building at midnight.`,explanation:`"Seen leaving" takes an -ing form.`,options:[`to leaving`,`leaving`,`left`,`leave`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`The passive of a perception verb can take -ing.`,0:`"To leaving" is ungrammatical.`,2:`A participle does not fit.`}},{id:`c1-vf-153`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`She spoke so quietly that hardly ___ in the audience could hear her.`,explanation:`"Hardly anyone" means almost nobody.`,options:[`someone`,`everyone`,`no one`,`anyone`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Hardly" has a negative sense.`,1:`"Hardly everyone" changes the meaning.`,2:`"Hardly no one" is a double negative.`}},{id:`c1-vf-154`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`We ___ have gone abroad for the holiday, but the cost of flights put us off.`,explanation:`"Could have" expresses a possibility that was not taken up.`,options:[`could`,`would rather`,`ought`,`had better`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Had better have gone" is not used.`,1:`"Would rather have gone" changes the structure.`,2:`"Ought have" lacks "to".`}},{id:`c1-vf-155`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`We are confident ___ the success of the project.`,explanation:`"Confident of" introduces the thing.`,options:[`of`,`at`,`to`,`for`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Confident to" is followed by a verb.`,3:`"Confident for" is not standard.`,1:`"Confident at" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-vf-156`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`By the time we got to the cinema, the film ___.`,explanation:`The past perfect shows an earlier action.`,options:[`was already beginning`,`has already begun`,`already began`,`had already begun`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`The past perfect shows an earlier action.`,1:`The present perfect does not fit.`,0:`The continuous does not show completion.`}},{id:`c1-vf-157`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`We were just ___ the town when the car broke down.`,explanation:`"Were just leaving" shows an action about to finish.`,options:[`left`,`to leave`,`leaving`,`leave`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Just leaving" shows an action in progress.`,0:`A participle needs an auxiliary.`,3:`"Were just leave" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-vf-158`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`She has two sisters, ___ of them are married.`,explanation:`"Both" refers to two.`,options:[`all`,`both`,`neither`,`either`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Either" means one of two.`,2:`"Neither of them are" has the opposite meaning.`,0:`"All" refers to more than two.`}},{id:`c1-vf-159`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`They are determined to stand ___ their principles whatever the cost.`,explanation:`"Stand by" means to remain loyal to.`,options:[`at`,`by`,`on`,`for`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Stand for" means to represent.`,2:`"Stand on" is not the idiom.`,0:`"Stand at" is not the idiom.`}},{id:`c1-vf-160`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`She ___ the manuscript for years before she finally found a publisher.`,explanation:`The past perfect continuous shows duration before a past event.`,options:[`had been revising`,`revised`,`has been revising`,`was revising`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`The present perfect does not fit.`,3:`The continuous does not show duration before the event.`,1:`The simple past does not show duration.`}},{id:`c1-vf-161`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Had the firm invested more in training, it ___ the skills shortage that now threatens its future.`,explanation:`The inverted third conditional uses "would have" + participle.`,options:[`would avoid`,`had avoided`,`will have avoided`,`would have avoided`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`This refers to the present.`,2:`"Will" does not fit an unreal past.`,1:`The result clause needs a modal.`}},{id:`c1-vf-162`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`By the time you read this letter, I ___ for Australia.`,explanation:`The future perfect shows an action completed before a future point.`,options:[`will leave`,`leave`,`have left`,`will have left`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`The future perfect shows completion before a future point.`,2:`The present perfect does not fit a future point.`,1:`A present form does not show completion.`}},{id:`c1-vf-163`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The conference will take place ___ the auspices of the university.`,explanation:`"Under the auspices of" means with the support of.`,options:[`by`,`under`,`at`,`in`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"In the auspices" is not standard.`,2:`"At the auspices" is not standard.`,0:`"By the auspices" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-vf-164`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`I ___ rather not say anything until I have spoken to my lawyer.`,explanation:`"Would rather" expresses a preference.`,options:[`would`,`could`,`should`,`will`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Will rather" is unusual.`,2:`"Should rather" is less common.`,1:`"Could rather" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-vf-165`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`He is regarded as ___ authority on medieval history.`,explanation:`"An authority" means one of several experts.`,options:[`some`,`an`,`no article`,`the`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"The" would imply a unique authority.`,2:`A singular noun needs a determiner.`,0:`"Some authority" has another meaning.`}},{id:`c1-vf-166`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`They were delighted ___ the results of the survey.`,explanation:`"Delighted with" introduces the thing.`,options:[`to`,`with`,`of`,`for`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Delighted of" is not standard.`,3:`"Delighted for" has another meaning.`,0:`"Delighted to" is followed by a verb.`}},{id:`c1-vf-167`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`He has been working there ___ the age of sixteen.`,explanation:`"Since" marks the starting point.`,options:[`from`,`by`,`since`,`for`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"For" needs a length of time.`,0:`"From" does not link to now.`,1:`"By" has a different meaning.`}},{id:`c1-vf-168`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`She ___ have been about thirty, but she looked older.`,explanation:`"Can't have" expresses a negative deduction.`,options:[`needn't`,`shouldn't`,`can't`,`mustn't`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Mustn't have" is not used for deduction.`,1:`"Shouldn't have" expresses criticism.`,0:`"Needn't have" has another meaning.`}},{id:`c1-vf-169`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Several passengers complained ___ the delay.`,explanation:`"Complain about" introduces the cause.`,options:[`at`,`about`,`for`,`of`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Complained of" is used for illness.`,0:`"Complained at" is not standard.`,2:`"Complained for" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-vf-170`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`When I was young, we ___ to the seaside every summer.`,explanation:`"Used to" expresses a past habit.`,options:[`have gone`,`used to go`,`use to go`,`were used to go`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`The past form is "used".`,3:`"Be used to" has another meaning.`,0:`The present perfect does not fit a past habit.`}},{id:`c1-vf-171`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`When I ___ home, I will phone you.`,explanation:`A present form follows a time conjunction.`,options:[`got`,`get`,`have got`,`will get`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`A future form is not used after "when".`,0:`A past form does not refer to the future.`,2:`The present perfect is less natural here.`}},{id:`c1-vf-172`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The two sides are still ___ odds over the terms of the agreement.`,explanation:`"At odds" means in disagreement.`,options:[`on`,`at`,`by`,`in`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"On odds" is not an idiom.`,3:`"In odds" is not an idiom.`,2:`"By odds" is not an idiom.`}},{id:`c1-vf-173`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The suspect is thought ___ left the country last week.`,explanation:`The perfect infinitive shows an earlier time.`,options:[`has`,`to has`,`to have`,`having`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"To" takes the base form.`,3:`A participle does not follow "thought".`,0:`A finite verb does not follow "thought".`}},{id:`c1-vf-174`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`The two sides have ___ in common except their desire to avoid war.`,explanation:`"Nothing ... except" excludes a single feature.`,options:[`everything`,`anything`,`something`,`nothing`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Have anything in common except" does not fit.`,2:`"Something ... except" changes the meaning.`,0:`"Everything ... except" changes the meaning.`}},{id:`c1-vf-175`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`You ___ better take an umbrella; it looks like rain.`,explanation:`"Had better" gives strong advice.`,options:[`should`,`have`,`would`,`had`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Would better" is not standard.`,0:`"Should better" is ungrammatical.`,1:`"Have better" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-vf-176`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`She ___ to be told twice; she always grasps instructions at once.`,explanation:`"Never needs to" is a regular present form.`,options:[`never need`,`never needs`,`never needing`,`doesn't needs`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`With "never" as the main negative the verb takes "-s".`,3:`"Doesn't" is followed by the base form.`,2:`A finite verb is needed.`}},{id:`c1-vf-177`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I ___ in this city for twenty years, and I still love it.`,explanation:`The present perfect links duration to now.`,options:[`live`,`had lived`,`have lived`,`lived`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`The simple past does not link to now.`,1:`The past perfect does not fit.`,0:`The present simple does not show duration.`}},{id:`c1-vf-178`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`Never before ___ such a magnificent view.`,explanation:`"Never before" requires inversion with an auxiliary.`,options:[`did I seen`,`had I seen`,`have I saw`,`I had seen`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`A negative adverb at the start needs inversion.`,2:`The participle is wrong.`,0:`This form is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-vf-179`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`Even if she ___ the lottery, she would still work.`,explanation:`"Even if" + past simple expresses an unreal situation.`,options:[`won`,`had won`,`would win`,`wins`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`The second conditional needs a past form.`,2:`"Would" is not used in the if-clause.`,1:`This refers to the past.`}},{id:`c1-vf-180`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The conference, ___ was held in a coastal resort, attracted delegates from fifty countries.`,explanation:`"Which" introduces a nonrestrictive clause.`,options:[`which`,`that`,`where`,`what`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"What" cannot introduce a relative clause.`,1:`"That" cannot follow a comma.`,2:`"Where" refers to place without a preposition.`}},{id:`c1-vf-181`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The author's ideas are open ___ criticism.`,explanation:`"Open to" means liable to receive.`,options:[`to`,`by`,`at`,`for`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Open for" has another meaning.`,2:`"Open at" is not standard.`,1:`"Open by" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-vf-182`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The two sisters, ___ of whom live abroad, visit us every summer.`,explanation:`"Both of whom" refers to two.`,options:[`both`,`all`,`neither`,`either`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"Either of whom" changes the meaning.`,1:`"All of whom" refers to more than two.`,2:`"Neither of whom live" has a negative sense.`}},{id:`c1-vf-183`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`The two companies have ___ in common except their size.`,explanation:`"Nothing ... except" excludes one feature.`,options:[`nothing`,`everything`,`anything`,`something`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Have anything in common except" does not fit the negative sense.`,3:`"Something in common except" changes the meaning.`,1:`"Everything in common except" changes the meaning.`}},{id:`c1-vf-184`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`She would have gone to the party if she ___ invited.`,explanation:`The if-clause uses the past perfect passive.`,options:[`would be`,`was`,`were`,`had been`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`The third conditional needs the past perfect.`,2:`This refers to the present.`,0:`"Would" is not used in the if-clause.`}},{id:`c1-vf-185`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`We are grateful ___ your generous support.`,explanation:`"Grateful for" introduces the thing.`,options:[`at`,`of`,`for`,`to`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Grateful of" is not standard.`,0:`"Grateful at" is not standard.`,3:`"Grateful to" introduces the person.`}},{id:`c1-vf-186`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`By the end of next year, the firm ___ its operations to three new countries.`,explanation:`"By the end of next year" calls for the future perfect.`,options:[`is expanding`,`had expanded`,`will have expanded`,`expands`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`A present tense does not fit.`,0:`The present continuous does not show completion.`,1:`The past perfect does not fit a future time.`}},{id:`c1-vf-187`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`This time tomorrow we ___ our final exam.`,explanation:`The future continuous shows an action in progress.`,options:[`will sit`,`sit`,`will be sitting`,`are sitting`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`The future continuous shows an action in progress.`,1:`A present tense does not fit.`,3:`The present continuous does not point to the future.`}},{id:`c1-vf-188`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The two candidates, ___ of whom had experience, were rejected.`,explanation:`"Neither of whom" refers to two.`,options:[`none`,`either`,`both`,`neither`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Either of whom had" would change the meaning.`,0:`"None" is used for more than two.`,2:`"Both" has the opposite meaning.`}},{id:`c1-vf-189`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I have not been able to concentrate ___ the news broke.`,explanation:`"Since" introduces a starting point.`,options:[`from`,`since`,`for`,`during`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"For" needs a length of time.`,0:`"From" does not combine with the present perfect.`,3:`"During" does not fit.`}},{id:`c1-vf-190`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The manager pointed ___ that the figures had been calculated incorrectly.`,explanation:`"Point out" means to draw attention to.`,options:[`out`,`up`,`at`,`off`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Point up" has a different sense.`,2:`"Point at" means to indicate with a finger.`,3:`"Point off" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-vf-191`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The new law will come ___ effect at the beginning of next year.`,explanation:`"Come into effect" is the standard collocation.`,options:[`on`,`at`,`in`,`into`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Come on effect" is not standard.`,1:`"Come at effect" is not standard.`,2:`"Come in effect" is less usual.`}},{id:`c1-vf-192`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`She was reprimanded ___ failing to report the incident promptly.`,explanation:`"Reprimanded for" introduces the reason.`,options:[`with`,`on`,`of`,`for`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Reprimanded of" is not standard.`,1:`"Reprimanded on" is not standard.`,0:`"Reprimanded with" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-vf-193`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`She was ___ upset by the news that she could not speak.`,explanation:`"So ... that" shows a result.`,options:[`so`,`such`,`too`,`very`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Such upset by" is ungrammatical.`,3:`"Very ... that" does not form a result clause.`,2:`"Too" does not take "that".`}},{id:`c1-vf-194`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The company is committed ___ reducing its emissions.`,explanation:`"Committed to" is followed by -ing.`,options:[`for`,`in`,`to`,`with`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Committed for" is not standard.`,3:`"Committed with" is not standard.`,1:`"Committed in" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-vf-195`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Few people ___ have predicted that the small start-up would overtake its larger rivals.`,explanation:`"Could have predicted" refers to a past ability.`,options:[`could`,`needn't`,`shan't`,`mustn't`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Needn't have predicted" says it was unnecessary.`,3:`"Mustn't have" is not used here.`,2:`"Shan't" is a refusal.`}},{id:`c1-vf-196`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`I shall go out whatever the weather ___.`,explanation:`A present form follows "whatever".`,options:[`was`,`will be`,`is`,`being`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`A future form is not used here.`,0:`A past form does not refer to the future.`,3:`A finite verb is needed.`}},{id:`c1-vf-197`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`The task proved ___ more difficult than we had imagined.`,explanation:`"Far" intensifies a comparative.`,options:[`very`,`so`,`far`,`too`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Very more" is ungrammatical.`,3:`"Too more" is ungrammatical.`,1:`"So more" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-vf-198`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`There is nothing ___ pleases her more than a well-organised event.`,explanation:`"That" is the usual relative after "nothing".`,options:[`who`,`that`,`which`,`what`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"What" cannot follow "nothing".`,2:`"Which" is less usual after "nothing".`,0:`"Who" refers to people.`}},{id:`c1-vf-199`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`We ___ a lot about the problem, but we have found no solution.`,explanation:`The present perfect links a period to now.`,options:[`had thought`,`think`,`thought`,`have thought`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`The present simple does not link to now.`,2:`The simple past does not link to now.`,0:`The past perfect does not fit.`}},{id:`c1-vf-200`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`It is recommended that all staff ___ the new safety procedures before the end of the month.`,explanation:`"Recommended that" takes the base form.`,options:[`reading`,`reads`,`will read`,`read`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`The mandative subjunctive uses the base form.`,2:`"Will" is not used here.`,0:`A finite verb is needed.`}},{id:`c1-vf-201`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`It ___ be pointed out that the figures were based on provisional data.`,explanation:`"Should be pointed out" is a formal way of drawing attention to a fact.`,options:[`had better`,`would rather`,`might as well`,`should`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Had better be pointed out" is informal.`,2:`"Might as well" does not fit a formal remark.`,1:`"Would rather" expresses preference.`}},{id:`c1-vf-202`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I ___ you last night, but you didn't answer your phone.`,explanation:`The simple past reports a completed action.`,options:[`have called`,`called`,`was calling`,`had called`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`A finished time reference needs the simple past.`,3:`The past perfect does not fit.`,2:`The continuous does not show a completed call.`}},{id:`c1-vf-203`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`She ___ have told us about the delay, but she said nothing.`,explanation:`"Might at least have" voices a mild reproach.`,options:[`ought`,`would rather`,`might at least`,`had better`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Had better have told" is not used.`,1:`"Would rather have told" changes the meaning.`,0:`"Ought have" lacks "to".`}},{id:`c1-vf-204`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`They were arrested on suspicion ___ fraud.`,explanation:`"Suspicion of" introduces the offence.`,options:[`of`,`for`,`with`,`at`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Suspicion for" is not standard.`,3:`"Suspicion at" is not standard.`,2:`"Suspicion with" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-vf-205`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The people with ___ I work are extremely friendly.`,explanation:`"With whom" follows the preposition.`,options:[`who`,`which`,`that`,`whom`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`After a preposition "whom" is used.`,1:`"Which" refers to things.`,2:`"That" cannot follow a preposition.`}},{id:`c1-vf-206`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The findings were ___ with those of previous studies.`,explanation:`"Consistent with" means in agreement with.`,options:[`consistently`,`constant`,`consistency`,`consistent`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`An adjective is needed.`,2:`"Consistency" is a noun.`,1:`"Constant" has another meaning.`}},{id:`c1-vf-207`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`The report, ___ findings were widely publicised, led to a change in policy.`,explanation:`"Whose" shows possession.`,options:[`which`,`its`,`what`,`whose`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Which findings" cannot show possession here.`,2:`"What findings" is ungrammatical.`,1:`"Its findings were" cannot join the clause directly.`}},{id:`c1-vf-208`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I ___ about the matter until you mentioned it just now.`,explanation:`The past perfect marks the state before a past moment.`,options:[`have not thought`,`had not thought`,`did not think`,`was not thinking`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`The past perfect shows an earlier state.`,0:`The present perfect does not fit "until just now".`,3:`The continuous does not fit.`}},{id:`c1-vf-209`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The government is bound ___ honour the terms of the treaty.`,explanation:`"Bound to" is followed by an infinitive.`,options:[`for`,`by`,`at`,`to`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Bound by honour" would change the structure.`,0:`"Bound for" means heading towards.`,2:`"Bound at" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-vf-210`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`At the moment I ___ a book on the history of the Roman Empire.`,explanation:`The present continuous shows an action in progress.`,options:[`was reading`,`read`,`have read`,`am reading`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`The present simple does not show an action in progress.`,2:`The present perfect shows completion.`,0:`The sentence is about the present.`}},{id:`c1-vf-211`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If you ___ me, I would have helped you.`,explanation:`The third conditional uses the past perfect.`,options:[`would ask`,`have asked`,`had asked`,`asked`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`The third conditional needs the past perfect.`,0:`"Would" is not used in the if-clause.`,1:`The past perfect is needed.`}},{id:`c1-vf-212`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`I haven't got ___ idea what he is talking about.`,explanation:`"The faintest idea" is a fixed phrase.`,options:[`faintly`,`a faint`,`faintest`,`the faintest`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`The superlative needs "the".`,1:`"A faint idea" has another meaning.`,0:`"Faintly" is an adverb.`}},{id:`c1-vf-213`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`She ___ well be right, but I still think the plan is risky.`,explanation:`"May well" expresses a strong possibility.`,options:[`must`,`should`,`would`,`may`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Would well be" has another meaning.`,1:`"Should well be" is unusual.`,0:`"Must well be" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-vf-214`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`The weather was ___ better than we had expected.`,explanation:`"Far" intensifies a comparative.`,options:[`very`,`too`,`more`,`far`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Very better" is ungrammatical.`,1:`"Too better" is ungrammatical.`,2:`"More better" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-vf-215`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`I would appreciate ___ if you could keep this confidential.`,explanation:`"I would appreciate it if" is a fixed polite structure.`,options:[`them`,`that`,`this`,`it`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"I would appreciate that if" is awkward.`,2:`"This" is less natural.`,0:`"Them" has no reference.`}},{id:`c1-vf-216`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`The lecture ___ at nine tomorrow; don't be late.`,explanation:`The present simple is used for timetables.`,options:[`will start`,`starts`,`is starting`,`started`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`The timetable calls for the present simple.`,3:`A past form does not refer to the future.`,2:`The continuous suggests an arrangement.`}},{id:`c1-vf-217`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`They ___ have lost their way; they know the area well.`,explanation:`"Can't have" expresses a negative deduction.`,options:[`shan't`,`can't`,`needn't`,`mustn't`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Mustn't have" is not used for deduction.`,0:`"Shan't have" is ungrammatical.`,2:`"Needn't have" has another meaning.`}},{id:`c1-vf-218`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The lecturer was greeted ___ a round of applause as he entered the hall.`,explanation:`"Greeted with" introduces the reaction.`,options:[`by`,`at`,`to`,`with`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Greeted by" would introduce the person who greeted him.`,1:`"Greeted at" is not standard.`,2:`"Greeted to" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-vf-219`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`She looked tired because she ___ all night.`,explanation:`The past perfect continuous explains an earlier cause.`,options:[`works`,`was working`,`has been working`,`had been working`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`The present perfect does not fit a past observation.`,1:`The simple continuous does not explain the earlier cause.`,0:`The sentence is in the past.`}},{id:`c1-vf-220`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`They ___ for the results since early morning and are beginning to feel anxious.`,explanation:`The present perfect continuous links duration to now.`,options:[`have been waiting`,`waited`,`are waiting`,`had been waiting`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`The simple past does not link to now.`,3:`The past perfect does not fit.`,2:`The present continuous does not show duration.`}},{id:`c1-vf-221`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`The report ___ by the time the committee met, so members had time to read it.`,explanation:`The past perfect passive shows an action before a past event.`,options:[`has been circulated`,`circulated`,`was circulated`,`had been circulated`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`The past perfect shows an earlier action.`,0:`The present perfect does not fit.`,1:`The report is the receiver.`}},{id:`c1-vf-222`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The speaker, ___ speech had been long, finally sat down.`,explanation:`"Whose" shows possession.`,options:[`whom`,`whose`,`who`,`which`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Who speech" is ungrammatical.`,0:`"Whom speech" is ungrammatical.`,3:`"Which speech" does not show possession.`}},{id:`c1-vf-223`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`Anyone ___ wishes to withdraw must notify the office in writing.`,explanation:`"Who" is the subject.`,options:[`which`,`whom`,`whose`,`who`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Whom" is an object form.`,0:`"Which" refers to things.`,2:`"Whose" shows possession.`}},{id:`c1-vf-224`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The two sides finally came ___ an agreement.`,explanation:`"Come to an agreement" is the standard collocation.`,options:[`to`,`on`,`at`,`into`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Come at an agreement" is not standard.`,3:`"Come into an agreement" is not standard.`,1:`"Come on an agreement" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-vf-225`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I ___ hardly sat down when the phone rang.`,explanation:`"Hardly had" shows an action just before another.`,options:[`had`,`was`,`did`,`have`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`The past perfect is needed.`,1:`"Was hardly sat" is ungrammatical.`,2:`"Did hardly sat" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-vf-226`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`I'd rather you ___ the matter to anyone until the announcement is made.`,explanation:`A past form expresses a present preference.`,options:[`won't mention`,`didn't mention`,`don't mention`,`haven't mentioned`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`After "would rather" + person use a past form.`,0:`"Won't" is not used here.`,3:`The present perfect does not fit.`}},{id:`c1-vf-227`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`They have an agreement ___ which neither side can withdraw.`,explanation:`"Withdraw from" is the standard collocation.`,options:[`to`,`by`,`of`,`from`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Agreement of which" is not standard.`,0:`"Agreement to which" is not standard.`,1:`"Agreement by which" has another meaning.`}},{id:`c1-vf-228`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Some of the manuscripts, ___ were centuries old, had been badly damaged.`,explanation:`"Which" introduces a nonrestrictive clause.`,options:[`which`,`whose`,`what`,`that`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"What" cannot introduce a relative clause.`,1:`"Whose" shows possession.`,3:`"That" cannot follow a comma.`}},{id:`c1-vf-229`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`She gave a ___ speech that moved the audience to tears.`,explanation:`"Poignant" means deeply moving.`,options:[`pungent`,`poignantly`,`poignancy`,`poignant`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`An adjective is needed.`,2:`"Poignancy" is a noun.`,0:`"Pungent" means strong-smelling.`}},{id:`c1-vf-230`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`I'd rather you ___ anything to him until we have confirmed the details.`,explanation:`A past form expresses a preference about someone else's action.`,options:[`won't say`,`don't say`,`didn't say`,`haven't said`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`After "would rather" + person use a past form.`,0:`"Won't" is not used here.`,3:`The present perfect does not fit.`}},{id:`c1-vf-231`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`The chairman made ___ of his remarks to a few key points.`,explanation:`"Make the most of" is a fixed phrase.`,options:[`the most`,`mostly`,`a most`,`most`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`The superlative needs "the".`,2:`"A most" has another meaning.`,1:`"Mostly" is an adverb.`}},{id:`c1-vf-232`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`He was convicted ___ fraud and sentenced to five years in prison.`,explanation:`"Convicted of" introduces the offence.`,options:[`with`,`for`,`on`,`of`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Convicted for" is not standard.`,0:`"Convicted with" is not standard.`,2:`"Convicted on" introduces the evidence.`}},{id:`c1-vf-233`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`By the time the rescue party arrived, the climbers ___ for three days.`,explanation:`The past perfect passive shows a state before a past event.`,options:[`had been trapped`,`have been trapped`,`trapped`,`were trapped`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`The past perfect shows duration before arrival.`,1:`The present perfect does not fit.`,2:`The climbers are the receivers.`}},{id:`c1-vf-234`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`I would never have guessed that he ___ so shy as a child.`,explanation:`The past perfect shows a state before the time of guessing.`,options:[`was`,`has been`,`had been`,`is`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`The past perfect shows an earlier state.`,1:`The present perfect does not fit.`,3:`A present tense does not fit.`}},{id:`c1-vf-235`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`If you ___ like to leave a message, please speak after the tone.`,explanation:`"Would like" is a polite form.`,options:[`can`,`should`,`will`,`would`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Will like" is less polite.`,1:`"Should like" is formal and rare.`,0:`"Can like" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-vf-236`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`He has ___ the slightest interest in politics.`,explanation:`"Not the slightest" is an emphatic negative.`,options:[`none`,`never`,`no`,`not`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"No the slightest" is ungrammatical.`,0:`"None the slightest" is ungrammatical.`,1:`"Never the slightest" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-vf-237`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Had we known about the strike, we ___ a different route.`,explanation:`The inverted third conditional uses "would have" + participle.`,options:[`had chosen`,`will choose`,`would choose`,`would have chosen`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`This refers to the present.`,1:`The conditional is unreal.`,0:`The result clause needs a modal.`}},{id:`c1-vf-238`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The hotel, ___ owners had refurbished it recently, was booked solid for the summer.`,explanation:`"Whose" shows possession.`,options:[`that`,`which`,`whose`,`of which`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Which owners" does not show possession here.`,0:`"That" cannot show possession.`,3:`"Of which owners" is awkward.`}},{id:`c1-vf-239`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`He answered all the questions ___ ease.`,explanation:`"With ease" means easily.`,options:[`with`,`in`,`by`,`at`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"By ease" is not standard.`,1:`"In ease" is not standard.`,3:`"At ease" has a different meaning.`}},{id:`c1-vf-240`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Hardly ___ of the proposals received any support.`,explanation:`"Hardly any" means almost none.`,options:[`all`,`any`,`some`,`no`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Hardly no" is a double negative.`,2:`"Hardly some" is not idiomatic.`,0:`"Hardly all" changes the meaning.`}},{id:`c1-vf-241`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`She ___ to have been the most talented student of her year.`,explanation:`"Be said to have been" is a reporting structure.`,options:[`says`,`is saying`,`has said`,`is said`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`The passive reporting structure is needed.`,1:`A continuous form changes the meaning.`,2:`The subject receives the reporting.`}},{id:`c1-vf-242`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`No sooner ___ she left the room than the arguing started again.`,explanation:`"No sooner had" is the standard inversion.`,options:[`did`,`was`,`had`,`has`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`The past perfect is needed.`,0:`"Did she left" is ungrammatical.`,1:`"Was she left" is passive.`}},{id:`c1-vf-243`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`The company ___ its profits by half before the new CEO arrived.`,explanation:`The past perfect shows an earlier change.`,options:[`halved`,`has halved`,`was halving`,`had halved`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`The earlier change needs the past perfect.`,1:`The present perfect does not fit.`,2:`The continuous does not show completion.`}},{id:`c1-vf-244`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The expedition, which ___ for months, was finally ready to depart.`,explanation:`The past perfect passive shows a process before departure.`,options:[`has been planned`,`was planned`,`had been planned`,`planned`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`The past perfect passive shows an earlier process.`,0:`The present perfect does not fit.`,3:`The expedition is the receiver.`}},{id:`c1-vf-245`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`He is ___ expert on the history of the region.`,explanation:`"An expert" means one of several.`,options:[`some`,`the`,`no article`,`an`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"The expert" suggests a unique person.`,2:`A singular noun needs a determiner.`,0:`"Some expert" has another meaning.`}},{id:`c1-vf-246`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`He was sentenced ___ death for treason.`,explanation:`"Sentenced to" introduces the punishment.`,options:[`for`,`by`,`to`,`at`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Sentenced at" is not standard.`,0:`"Sentenced for death" is not standard.`,1:`"Sentenced by" introduces an agent.`}},{id:`c1-vf-247`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`They ___ have left yet; their coats are still here.`,explanation:`"Can't have" expresses a negative deduction.`,options:[`needn't`,`can't`,`shouldn't`,`mustn't`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Mustn't have" is not used for deduction.`,0:`"Needn't have" has another meaning.`,2:`"Shouldn't have" expresses criticism.`}},{id:`c1-vf-248`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`I'll lend you the car provided you ___ it back by six.`,explanation:`A present form follows "provided".`,options:[`brought`,`will bring`,`bring`,`brings`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`A future form is not used after "provided".`,0:`A past form does not refer to the future.`,3:`"You" takes the base form.`}},{id:`c1-vf-249`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`It is high time you ___ the truth about what happened.`,explanation:`"High time" + past simple states that action is overdue.`,options:[`told`,`tell`,`have told`,`will tell`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`After "it is high time" a past form is used.`,3:`"Will" is not used here.`,2:`The present perfect does not fit.`}},{id:`c1-vf-250`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`The moment the doors ___, a crowd rushed into the store.`,explanation:`The simple past reports the sequence.`,options:[`have opened`,`had opened`,`opened`,`were opening`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`The two actions happened almost together.`,0:`The present perfect does not fit.`,3:`The continuous does not fit a sudden event.`}},{id:`c1-vf-251`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`She will be tired when she ___ home tonight.`,explanation:`A present form follows a time conjunction.`,options:[`getting`,`gets`,`got`,`will get`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`A future form is not used after "when".`,2:`A past form does not refer to the future.`,0:`A finite verb is needed.`}},{id:`c1-vf-252`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`We ___ the summit by dusk if the weather had not turned against us.`,explanation:`The third conditional result uses "would have" + participle.`,options:[`will have reached`,`had reached`,`would have reached`,`would reach`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`This refers to the present.`,1:`The result clause needs a modal.`,0:`"Will" does not fit an unreal past.`}},{id:`c1-vf-253`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`You ___ not have come; I could have managed alone.`,explanation:`"Need not have" says an action was unnecessary.`,options:[`must`,`needn't`,`need`,`should`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Needn't not" is a double negative.`,0:`"Must not have" is not used here.`,3:`"Should not have" expresses criticism.`}},{id:`c1-vf-254`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`She ___ in the same house all her life.`,explanation:`The present perfect links a period to now.`,options:[`lived`,`lives`,`has lived`,`had lived`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`The simple past does not link to now.`,3:`The past perfect does not fit.`,1:`The present simple does not show duration.`}},{id:`c1-vf-255`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`They ___ to have returned to the capital by now, but nobody has seen them.`,explanation:`"Ought to have" expresses an expectation.`,options:[`must`,`should to`,`would`,`ought`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Should" is not followed by "to".`,0:`"Must to have" is ungrammatical.`,2:`"Would to have" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-vf-256`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`No sooner ___ the new law come into force than it was challenged in the courts.`,explanation:`"No sooner had" is the standard inversion.`,options:[`has`,`was`,`did`,`had`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`The past perfect is needed.`,2:`"Did the new law come" lacks the correct pair.`,1:`"Was the new law come" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-vf-257`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I have been working on this project ___ last September.`,explanation:`"Since" introduces a starting point.`,options:[`from`,`during`,`for`,`since`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"For" needs a length of time.`,0:`"From" does not combine with the present perfect.`,1:`"During" does not fit.`}},{id:`c1-vf-258`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`If I ___ able to choose again, I would take exactly the same decision.`,explanation:`"If I were able" is an unreal present condition.`,options:[`am`,`would be`,`will be`,`were`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Would" is not used in the if-clause.`,2:`"Will" is not used in the if-clause.`,0:`The second conditional needs a past form.`}},{id:`c1-vf-259`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The book, ___ I read several years ago, changed the way I think about history.`,explanation:`"Which" introduces a nonrestrictive clause.`,options:[`that`,`which`,`whose`,`what`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"What" cannot introduce a relative clause.`,0:`"That" cannot follow a comma.`,2:`"Whose" shows possession.`}},{id:`c1-vf-260`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`The performance was ___ disappointing that many left early.`,explanation:`"So ... that" shows a result.`,options:[`so`,`such`,`too`,`very`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Such disappointing" lacks a noun.`,3:`"Very ... that" does not form a result clause.`,2:`"Too" does not take "that".`}},{id:`c1-vf-261`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I ___ you a postcard when I was in Rome, but I forgot to post it.`,explanation:`The simple past reports a completed action.`,options:[`have written`,`wrote`,`was writing`,`had written`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`The present perfect does not fit a finished past.`,2:`The continuous does not show completion.`,3:`The past perfect does not fit.`}},{id:`c1-vf-262`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`He was ___ of stealing the money, but there was no evidence.`,explanation:`"Accused of" introduces the alleged crime.`,options:[`excused`,`refused`,`accused`,`abused`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Excused" means forgiven.`,1:`"Refused" means declined.`,3:`"Abused" means mistreated.`}},{id:`c1-vf-263`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`There is ___ little evidence that the treatment is effective.`,explanation:`"Very little" emphasises the scarcity.`,options:[`too`,`very`,`a`,`so`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"A little evidence" would be positive.`,3:`"So little evidence" needs a result clause.`,0:`"Too little evidence" needs "for" or "to".`}},{id:`c1-vf-264`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The coach, ___ journey takes about nine hours, leaves at midnight.`,explanation:`"Whose" shows possession.`,options:[`of which`,`which`,`whose`,`that`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Which journey" does not show possession.`,3:`"That" cannot show possession.`,0:`"Of which journey" is awkward.`}},{id:`c1-vf-265`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`I wonder ___ of the two options is likely to be more successful.`,explanation:`"Which" chooses from a limited set.`,options:[`whose`,`what`,`who`,`which`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"What" does not choose between two.`,2:`"Who" refers to people.`,0:`"Whose" shows possession.`}},{id:`c1-vf-266`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Little ___ they know that their surprise was about to be ruined.`,explanation:`"Little did they know" is an inverted structure.`,options:[`did`,`do`,`have`,`were`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Little" at the start requires inversion with the past.`,2:`"Have" does not fit the past narrative.`,3:`"Were they know" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-vf-267`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`Not until much later ___ the full extent of her kindness.`,explanation:`"Not until" requires inversion with "did".`,options:[`he realised`,`had he realised`,`he did realise`,`did he realise`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Not until" at the start requires inversion.`,2:`The word order is not inverted.`,1:`The main clause is simple past here.`}},{id:`c1-vf-268`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`The jury was ___ in finding the defendant guilty.`,explanation:`"Unanimous" means in complete agreement.`,options:[`unaware`,`anonymous`,`unanimously`,`unanimous`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"Anonymous" means unnamed.`,2:`An adjective is needed.`,0:`"Unaware" means not knowing.`}},{id:`c1-vf-269`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`The soup tastes ___ without salt.`,explanation:`"Bland" means lacking flavour.`,options:[`blandly`,`blind`,`blandness`,`bland`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`After "taste" an adjective is used.`,2:`"Blandness" is a noun.`,1:`"Blind" means unable to see.`}},{id:`c1-vf-270`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`It is ___ a pity that you cannot join us.`,explanation:`"Such a pity" precedes a noun phrase.`,options:[`such`,`so`,`too`,`very`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"So a pity" is ungrammatical.`,3:`"Very a pity" is ungrammatical.`,2:`"Too a pity" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-vf-271`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The students, all of ___ passed the test, were awarded certificates.`,explanation:`"All of whom" is a standard relative construction.`,options:[`them`,`who`,`whom`,`which`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Which" refers to things.`,1:`After "of" the object form is needed.`,0:`"Them" cannot introduce a relative clause.`}},{id:`c1-vf-272`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`It is not the money ___ matters but the principle.`,explanation:`"That" introduces a cleft clause.`,options:[`that`,`whose`,`which`,`what`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"What" cannot follow a noun.`,2:`"Which" is less natural in a cleft.`,1:`"Whose" shows possession.`}},{id:`c1-vf-273`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`They ___ in Paris since 2015 and have no plans to leave.`,explanation:`The present perfect links "since" to now.`,options:[`live`,`had lived`,`have lived`,`lived`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`The simple past does not link to now.`,1:`The past perfect does not fit.`,0:`The present simple does not show duration.`}},{id:`c1-vf-274`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Each of the contestants ___ asked to introduce themselves.`,explanation:`"Each" takes a singular verb.`,options:[`were`,`are`,`was`,`being`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Each" takes a singular verb.`,1:`"are" does not work here, because "Each" takes a singular verb.`,3:`A finite verb is needed.`}},{id:`c1-vf-275`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`The report ___ before the board meets on Thursday.`,explanation:`The future perfect passive shows completion before a future time.`,options:[`will have been completed`,`has been completed`,`is completed`,`will be completed`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`The future perfect passive shows completion before a point.`,1:`The present perfect does not fit a future point.`,2:`A present tense does not show completion.`}},{id:`c1-vf-276`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`We ___ to have finished by six, but we are running late.`,explanation:`"Ought to have" expresses an expectation.`,options:[`ought`,`would`,`should to`,`must`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Should" is not followed by "to".`,3:`"Must to have" is ungrammatical.`,1:`"Would to have" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-vf-277`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`I wish you ___ stop interrupting me.`,explanation:`"Wish ... would" expresses annoyance.`,options:[`should`,`will`,`would`,`could to`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Wish ... will" is ungrammatical.`,3:`"Could to" is ungrammatical.`,0:`"Wish ... should" is unusual.`}},{id:`c1-vf-278`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The success of the venture depends largely ___ the goodwill of local people.`,explanation:`"Depend on" is the fixed collocation.`,options:[`at`,`on`,`of`,`from`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Depend of" is not standard.`,3:`"Depend from" is not standard.`,0:`"Depend at" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-vf-279`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`She would have been promoted had she ___ the deadline.`,explanation:`"Had she not missed" is the correct form.`,options:[`not missing`,`not miss`,`not missed`,`not have missed`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`The inverted conditional needs a participle.`,0:`A participle needs an auxiliary.`,3:`"Had she not have" is nonstandard.`}},{id:`c1-vf-280`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`Despite his best efforts, he failed to come to terms ___ the loss of his job.`,explanation:`"Come to terms with" means to accept.`,options:[`at`,`for`,`to`,`with`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Come to terms to" is not standard.`,0:`"Come to terms at" is not standard.`,1:`"Come to terms for" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-vf-281`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If I ___ you, I would think twice.`,explanation:`"If I were you" is the standard advice phrase.`,options:[`am`,`would be`,`was`,`were`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`The subjunctive "were" is used in formal English.`,0:`A present form does not fit an unreal condition.`,1:`"Would" is not used in the if-clause.`}},{id:`c1-vf-282`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`By the end of the month, the two companies ___ completely.`,explanation:`"By the end of the month" calls for the future perfect.`,options:[`merge`,`will have merged`,`will merge`,`have merged`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`The future perfect shows completion.`,0:`A present tense does not fit.`,3:`The present perfect does not fit.`}},{id:`c1-vf-283`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`When I get home, I ___ a hot bath.`,explanation:`"Will" predicts a future action.`,options:[`have`,`will have`,`am having`,`had`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`A present tense does not predict.`,2:`The continuous suggests an arrangement.`,3:`A past form does not refer to the future.`}},{id:`c1-vf-284`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`I would like to thank ___ who helped to organise the event.`,explanation:`"Everyone" refers to all people.`,options:[`anything`,`everyone`,`every one`,`everything`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Everything" refers to things.`,2:`"Every one" refers to one of a group.`,0:`"Anything" refers to things.`}},{id:`c1-vf-285`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`It was Maria ___ first noticed the error.`,explanation:`"Who" is the subject of the cleft clause.`,options:[`which`,`whose`,`whom`,`who`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Whom" is an object form.`,1:`"Whose" shows possession.`,0:`"Which" refers to things.`}},{id:`c1-vf-286`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`The firm ___ its profits steadily for five years before the recession hit.`,explanation:`The past perfect continuous shows duration before a past event.`,options:[`had been increasing`,`increased`,`was increasing`,`has been increasing`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`The past perfect continuous shows duration before the event.`,3:`The present perfect does not fit.`,1:`The simple past does not show duration.`}},{id:`c1-vf-287`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The ferry, ___ crossing normally takes forty minutes, was delayed by fog.`,explanation:`"Whose" shows possession.`,options:[`of which`,`which`,`whose`,`that`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Which crossing" does not show possession.`,3:`"That" cannot follow a comma.`,0:`"Of which crossing" is awkward.`}},{id:`c1-vf-288`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The decision, ___ was taken without consultation, provoked a wave of protest.`,explanation:`"Which" introduces a nonrestrictive clause.`,options:[`that`,`which`,`whose`,`what`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"What" cannot introduce a relative clause.`,0:`"That" cannot follow a comma.`,2:`"Whose" shows possession.`}},{id:`c1-vf-289`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`It was the ___ news we had ever received.`,explanation:`"Best" is the superlative of "good".`,options:[`well`,`good`,`best`,`better`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`A comparative does not fit "ever".`,0:`"Well news" is ungrammatical.`,1:`A superlative is needed.`}},{id:`c1-vf-290`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`The company ___ steadily since the new management took over.`,explanation:`The present perfect continuous links change to now.`,options:[`improved`,`has been improving`,`is improving`,`improves`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`The present continuous does not show duration since a point.`,0:`The simple past does not connect to now.`,3:`The present simple does not show change.`}},{id:`c1-vf-291`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`There are two options; ___ of them is perfect.`,explanation:`"Neither" refers to two.`,options:[`any`,`none`,`neither`,`all`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"None" is used for more than two.`,0:`"Any" does not give a negative sense.`,3:`"All" refers to more than two.`}},{id:`c1-vf-292`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`There is no point ___ the matter any further.`,explanation:`"No point in" is followed by a gerund.`,options:[`in discussing`,`to discuss`,`of discussing`,`at discussing`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"No point" is followed by "in" + gerund.`,2:`"Of" is not used here.`,3:`"At" is not used here.`}},{id:`c1-vf-293`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`Had the board acted sooner, the company ___ in such difficulties now.`,explanation:`A mixed conditional links a past condition to a present result.`,options:[`would not have been`,`will not be`,`would not be`,`was not`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Will" does not fit the unreal condition.`,0:`The result refers to the present.`,3:`A past form does not fit the mixed conditional.`}},{id:`c1-vf-294`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`The two proposals are very ___; there is little to choose between them.`,explanation:`"Similar" means alike.`,options:[`similarity`,`simulated`,`similar`,`simultaneous`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Simulated" means imitated.`,0:`"Similarity" is a noun.`,3:`"Simultaneous" means at the same time.`}},{id:`c1-vf-295`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`The negotiators ___ since early morning and show no signs of reaching agreement.`,explanation:`The present perfect continuous shows duration up to now.`,options:[`are talking`,`had been talking`,`have been talking`,`talked`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`The simple past does not link to now.`,1:`The past perfect does not fit.`,0:`The present continuous does not show duration.`}},{id:`c1-vf-296`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`We ___ before the plane took off.`,explanation:`The past perfect shows an earlier action.`,options:[`have boarded`,`boarded`,`were boarding`,`had boarded`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`The past perfect shows an earlier action.`,0:`The sentence is in the past.`,2:`The continuous does not show completion.`}},{id:`c1-vf-297`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`If the authorities ___ earlier, the outbreak could have been contained.`,explanation:`The if-clause uses the past perfect.`,options:[`act`,`would act`,`acted`,`had acted`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`The third conditional needs the past perfect.`,1:`"Would" is not used in the if-clause.`,0:`A present form does not fit.`}},{id:`c1-vf-298`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`She has ___ little money that she can hardly afford the rent.`,explanation:`"So little ... that" shows a result.`,options:[`too`,`such`,`so`,`very`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Such little money" is unnatural.`,3:`"Very ... that" does not form a result clause.`,0:`"Too" does not take "that".`}},{id:`c1-vf-299`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`You ___ not have gone to so much trouble; a simple meal would have been perfectly adequate.`,explanation:`"Need not have" says it was unnecessary but done.`,options:[`must`,`would`,`should to`,`need`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Must not have" is not used here.`,2:`"Should" is not followed by "to".`,1:`"Would not have gone" changes the meaning.`}},{id:`c1-vf-300`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`It was not until later ___ she realised the full extent of the damage.`,explanation:`"It was not until ... that" is a cleft structure.`,options:[`what`,`that`,`when`,`which`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"It was not until later when" is informal.`,3:`"Which" needs an antecedent.`,0:`"What" is ungrammatical here.`}},{id:`c1-vf-301`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If only we ___ more time, we could finish the project.`,explanation:`"If only" + past simple expresses a present wish.`,options:[`would have`,`had`,`did have`,`have`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"If only" + past expresses a present wish.`,0:`"Would" is not used here.`,2:`"Did have" is unusual.`}},{id:`c1-vf-302`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`He has made ___ progress in his studies this term.`,explanation:`"Considerable" means large.`,options:[`considerably`,`considerable`,`considerate`,`considering`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`An adjective is needed.`,2:`"Considerate" means thoughtful.`,3:`"Considering" is a participle.`}},{id:`c1-vf-303`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`___ I known what was going to happen, I would have stayed at home.`,explanation:`"Had" + subject + participle is the inverted third conditional.`,options:[`Had`,`Have`,`Did`,`Would`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`The inverted third conditional uses "had".`,2:`"Did" does not form this conditional.`,3:`"Would I known" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-vf-304`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`I'd like to thank everyone ___ made this evening possible.`,explanation:`"Who" is the subject of the relative clause.`,options:[`who`,`which`,`whose`,`whom`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`The relative acts as the subject.`,2:`"Whose" shows possession.`,1:`"Which" refers to things.`}},{id:`c1-vf-305`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`You ___ better see a doctor about that cough.`,explanation:`"Had better" gives strong advice.`,options:[`have`,`would`,`had`,`should`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Would better" is not standard.`,3:`"Should better" is ungrammatical.`,0:`"Have better" lacks the past form.`}},{id:`c1-vf-306`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`The ruins, ___ date from the first century, are among the best preserved in the region.`,explanation:`"Which" introduces a nonrestrictive clause.`,options:[`that`,`whose`,`what`,`which`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"That" cannot follow a comma.`,2:`"What" cannot introduce a relative clause.`,1:`"Whose" shows possession.`}},{id:`c1-vf-307`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The suspect is said ___ been seen near the scene.`,explanation:`The perfect infinitive shows an earlier time.`,options:[`to have`,`to has`,`has`,`having`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"To" takes the base form.`,3:`A participle does not follow "said".`,2:`A finite verb does not follow "said".`}},{id:`c1-vf-308`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`The new law will ___ all drivers, whatever their age.`,explanation:`"Affect" means to influence.`,options:[`afflict`,`effect`,`affect`,`infect`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Effect" as a verb means to bring about.`,0:`"Afflict" means to cause suffering.`,3:`"Infect" means to contaminate.`}},{id:`c1-vf-309`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`There was ___ food left after the party, so we threw it away.`,explanation:`"No" is a negative determiner.`,options:[`any`,`not`,`none`,`no`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"None food" is ungrammatical.`,1:`"Not food" is ungrammatical.`,0:`"Any" does not give a negative sense.`}},{id:`c1-vf-310`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`The committee reached a ___ decision after a long debate.`,explanation:`"Unanimous" means in full agreement.`,options:[`unanimous`,`unanimously`,`uniform`,`anonymous`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`An adjective is needed.`,3:`"Anonymous" means unnamed.`,2:`"Uniform" means identical.`}},{id:`c1-vf-311`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`You ___ well be right, but I am not convinced.`,explanation:`"May well" expresses a strong possibility.`,options:[`will`,`may`,`shall`,`would`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Will well" is ungrammatical.`,3:`"Would well" is unusual.`,2:`"Shall well" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-vf-312`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If it ___ not for his help, I would have failed.`,explanation:`"If it had not been for" means without.`,options:[`was`,`would be`,`had been`,`were`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`The third conditional needs the past perfect.`,3:`This refers to a present unreal situation.`,1:`"Would" is not used in the if-clause.`}},{id:`c1-vf-313`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The company is committed ___ reducing its carbon emissions by half.`,explanation:`"Committed to" is followed by -ing.`,options:[`with`,`for`,`to`,`in`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Committed for" is not standard.`,0:`"Committed with" is not standard.`,3:`"Committed in" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-vf-314`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`Visitors ___ not feed the animals; it is strictly forbidden.`,explanation:`"May not" expresses prohibition in formal rules.`,options:[`would`,`may`,`might`,`could`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Might not" is weaker.`,3:`"Could not" expresses inability.`,0:`"Would not" is conditional.`}},{id:`c1-vf-315`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`I ___ rather you didn't mention the matter to anyone for the time being.`,explanation:`"I would rather you didn't" is a polite request.`,options:[`would`,`might`,`had`,`should`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Had rather" is dated.`,3:`"Should rather" is rare.`,1:`"Might rather" is not used.`}},{id:`c1-vf-316`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`It was not until I met her ___ I realised how much I had missed my family.`,explanation:`"It was not until ... that" is a cleft structure.`,options:[`that`,`what`,`when`,`which`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"It was not until ... when" is informal.`,3:`"Which" is ungrammatical here.`,1:`"What" is ungrammatical here.`}},{id:`c1-vf-317`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`The committee has ___ authority to approve spending of this size.`,explanation:`"No" means not any.`,options:[`none`,`no`,`not`,`any`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Not authority" is ungrammatical.`,0:`"None authority" is ungrammatical.`,3:`"Has any authority" does not express denial.`}},{id:`c1-vf-318`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`He ___ his career as a clerk before rising to the top of the company.`,explanation:`The simple past reports events in sequence.`,options:[`began`,`had begun`,`has begun`,`begins`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`The simple past reports the sequence.`,2:`The present perfect does not fit.`,3:`The sentence is in the past.`}},{id:`c1-vf-319`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`The delegates were given ___ chance to express their views.`,explanation:`"No chance" is a negative determiner.`,options:[`no`,`not`,`none`,`any`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Not chance" is ungrammatical.`,2:`"None chance" is ungrammatical.`,3:`"Any" does not give a negative sense.`}},{id:`c1-vf-320`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`He gave ___ the impression of someone who had nothing to hide.`,explanation:`"Give someone the impression" takes an indirect object directly.`,options:[`with us`,`for us`,`us`,`to us`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Give us the impression" does not take "to".`,1:`"For us" is not idiomatic here.`,0:`"With us" is not idiomatic here.`}},{id:`c1-vf-321`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`The management ___ the staff that no redundancies were planned.`,explanation:`"Assure someone that" means to promise.`,options:[`insured`,`ensured`,`assured`,`reassured`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Ensure" does not take a personal object like this.`,0:`"Insure" is about insurance.`,3:`"Reassured the staff" changes the structure.`}},{id:`c1-vf-322`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`She said she ___ the film twice before.`,explanation:`The past perfect shows an earlier action in reported speech.`,options:[`had seen`,`sees`,`saw`,`has seen`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`The reporting verb is past.`,2:`The past perfect shows an earlier action.`,1:`The sentence is in the past.`}},{id:`c1-vf-323`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I ___ you a lift if you had asked me.`,explanation:`The third conditional uses "would have" + participle.`,options:[`will give`,`would give`,`would have given`,`had given`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`This refers to the present.`,3:`The result clause needs a modal.`,0:`"Will" does not fit an unreal past.`}},{id:`c1-vf-324`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`She did not have ___ idea what the instructions meant.`,explanation:`"Not the faintest idea" is a fixed phrase.`,options:[`a faint`,`the faintest`,`faintest`,`faintly`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`The superlative needs "the".`,0:`"A faint idea" has another meaning.`,3:`"Faintly" is an adverb.`}},{id:`c1-vf-325`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`They have been engaged ___ a bitter dispute for months.`,explanation:`"Engaged in" a dispute is the standard collocation.`,options:[`with`,`at`,`in`,`on`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Engaged at" is not standard.`,3:`"Engaged on" is not standard.`,0:`"Engaged with" has a different meaning.`}},{id:`c1-vf-326`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`She would have called you if she ___ your number.`,explanation:`The if-clause uses the past perfect.`,options:[`have had`,`has`,`would have`,`had had`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`The third conditional needs the past perfect.`,0:`"Have had" is not the past perfect.`,2:`"Would" is not used in the if-clause.`}},{id:`c1-vf-327`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`The lecture ___ have finished by now, so let's go and meet them.`,explanation:`"Should have" expresses an expectation.`,options:[`shan't`,`should`,`mustn't`,`needn't`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Mustn't have" is not used here.`,3:`"Needn't have" has another meaning.`,0:`"Shan't have" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-vf-328`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`If governments ___ earlier, the worst effects of climate change could have been avoided.`,explanation:`The if-clause uses the past perfect.`,options:[`act`,`acted`,`would act`,`had acted`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`The third conditional needs the past perfect.`,2:`"Would" is not used in the if-clause.`,0:`A present form does not fit.`}},{id:`c1-vf-329`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`conditionals`,topics:[`conditionals-wishes`],prompt:`If I ___ known about the problem, I would have said something.`,explanation:`The third conditional uses "had" + participle.`,options:[`have`,`had`,`did`,`would have`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`The third conditional needs the past perfect.`,3:`"Would" is not used in the if-clause.`,2:`"Did known" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-vf-330`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`It is not ___ my power to change the rules.`,explanation:`"Within my power" means possible for me.`,options:[`under`,`inside`,`within`,`on`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Inside my power" is not idiomatic.`,0:`"Under my power" has another meaning.`,3:`"On my power" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-vf-331`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The prisoners were not allowed ___ visitors.`,explanation:`The passive of "allow" takes "to".`,options:[`receiving`,`receive`,`received`,`to receive`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Allowed" takes an infinitive in the passive.`,1:`The passive requires "to".`,2:`An infinitive is needed.`}},{id:`c1-vf-332`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`adjectives`,topics:[`adjectives-adverbs`],prompt:`The proposal is ___ to be accepted, since few people support it.`,explanation:`"Unlikely" means improbable.`,options:[`likelihood`,`unliked`,`unlikely`,`unlike`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`"Unlike" is a preposition.`,1:`"Unliked" means not liked.`,0:`"Likelihood" is a noun.`}},{id:`c1-vf-333`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`As ___ result of the delay, several flights were rescheduled.`,explanation:`"As a result of" is a fixed phrase.`,options:[`an`,`–`,`the`,`a`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"The result" would require a specific, known result.`,0:`"Result" begins with a consonant sound.`,1:`A singular noun needs a determiner.`}},{id:`c1-vf-334`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`It is high time the company ___ its recruitment procedures.`,explanation:`"High time" + past simple states that action is overdue.`,options:[`will review`,`has reviewed`,`reviewed`,`reviews`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{3:`After "it is high time" a past form is used.`,0:`"Will" is not used here.`,1:`The present perfect does not fit.`}},{id:`c1-vf-335`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`work-education-technology`],prompt:`She is regarded ___ one of the most gifted engineers of her generation.`,explanation:`"Regard as" is the fixed pattern.`,options:[`to`,`for`,`like`,`as`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Regarded like" is not standard.`,0:`"Regarded to" is not standard.`,1:`"Regarded for" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-vf-336`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`I cannot think of anyone ___ opinion I respect more.`,explanation:`"Whose" shows possession.`,options:[`whom`,`whose`,`who`,`which`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Who opinion" is ungrammatical.`,0:`"Whom opinion" is ungrammatical.`,3:`"Which opinion" does not show possession for a person.`}},{id:`c1-vf-337`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The findings have important ___ for the treatment of the disease.`,explanation:`"Implications" are possible consequences.`,options:[`implications`,`applications`,`implementations`,`explanations`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Applications" refers to uses.`,2:`"Implementations" refers to putting into effect.`,3:`"Explanations" gives reasons.`}},{id:`c1-vf-338`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The firm hired several consultants, none of ___ had any experience of the industry.`,explanation:`"None of whom" is a standard relative construction.`,options:[`them`,`who`,`whom`,`which`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`After "of" the object form is needed.`,3:`"Which" refers to things.`,0:`"Them" cannot introduce a relative clause.`}},{id:`c1-vf-339`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The report, ___ findings were published last week, has prompted calls for reform.`,explanation:`"Whose" shows possession.`,options:[`of which`,`that`,`which`,`whose`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Which findings" does not show possession.`,1:`"That" cannot follow a comma.`,0:`"Of which findings" is awkward.`}},{id:`c1-vf-340`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`By the time she got home, her husband ___ dinner.`,explanation:`The past perfect shows an earlier action.`,options:[`was cooked`,`had cooked`,`cooked`,`has cooked`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`The sentence is in the past.`,2:`The simple past does not show an earlier action.`,0:`"Cook" is active here.`}},{id:`c1-vf-341`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`No sooner had the vaccine been introduced ___ the incidence of the disease fell dramatically.`,explanation:`"No sooner ... than" is a correlative pair.`,options:[`than`,`that`,`as`,`when`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"No sooner" is followed by "than".`,1:`"That" does not complete the pair.`,2:`"As" does not complete the pair.`}},{id:`c1-vf-342`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`This is the first time she ___ abroad on her own.`,explanation:`"This is the first time" is followed by the present perfect.`,options:[`is travelling`,`had travelled`,`travelled`,`has travelled`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"The first time" takes the present perfect.`,0:`The present continuous does not fit.`,1:`The past perfect does not fit.`}},{id:`c1-vf-343`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`By the time the doctor arrives, the patient ___ unconscious for ten minutes.`,explanation:`The future perfect gives a total up to a future point.`,options:[`will have been`,`will be`,`has been`,`is`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`The future perfect gives the total duration.`,2:`The present perfect does not fit a future point.`,3:`A present form does not give the total.`}},{id:`c1-vf-344`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`They have been at loggerheads ___ the question of pay for months.`,explanation:`"Over" introduces the matter of dispute.`,options:[`at`,`by`,`to`,`over`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"At loggerheads at" is not standard.`,1:`"At loggerheads by" is not standard.`,2:`"At loggerheads to" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-vf-345`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The children, none of ___ had been to a theatre, were thrilled.`,explanation:`"None of whom" is a standard relative construction.`,options:[`whom`,`them`,`which`,`who`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Which" refers to things.`,3:`After "of" the object form is needed.`,1:`"Them" cannot introduce a relative clause.`}},{id:`c1-vf-346`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`The board, ___ had been in session for hours, finally reached a decision.`,explanation:`"Which" introduces a nonrestrictive clause.`,options:[`which`,`who`,`what`,`whose`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Who" is used for individuals.`,3:`"Whose" shows possession.`,2:`"What" cannot introduce a relative clause.`}},{id:`c1-vf-347`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`confusing-words`,topics:[`confusing-words-word-formation`],prompt:`The ___ of the building took three years.`,explanation:`"Construction" is the process.`,options:[`construction`,`constructive`,`constructed`,`constructing`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Constructive" is an adjective.`,3:`"Constructing" is a participle.`,2:`"Constructed" is a participle.`}},{id:`c1-vf-348`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`This is the second time this week that the system ___ down.`,explanation:`"This is the second time" is followed by the present perfect.`,options:[`had gone`,`goes`,`went`,`has gone`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"The second time this week" takes the present perfect.`,0:`The past perfect does not fit.`,1:`The present simple does not fit.`}},{id:`c1-vf-349`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I'll let you know the minute I ___ anything.`,explanation:`A present form follows a time conjunction.`,options:[`hear`,`am hearing`,`will hear`,`heard`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`A future form is not used after "the minute".`,3:`A past form does not refer to the future.`,1:`The continuous is unnatural here.`}},{id:`c1-vf-350`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`Never before ___ so many people been displaced by conflict in the region.`,explanation:`"Never before" requires inversion.`,options:[`did`,`has`,`have`,`had`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"So many people" is plural.`,3:`"Never before" with the present perfect needs "have".`,0:`"Did so many people been" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-vf-351`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`You ___ have bothered to cook; we were going to eat out anyway.`,explanation:`"Needn't have" says an action was unnecessary.`,options:[`mustn't`,`needn't`,`shan't`,`can't`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Mustn't have" is not used here.`,3:`"Can't have bothered" expresses deduction.`,2:`"Shan't have" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-vf-352`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`The proposal strikes me as sensible, and I ___ add that it is also remarkably inexpensive.`,explanation:`"Might add" introduces a further point tentatively.`,options:[`might`,`have`,`will to`,`being`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`"Will" is not followed by "to" here.`,1:`"Have add" is ungrammatical.`,3:`A participle does not fit.`}},{id:`c1-vf-353`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`The doctor said I ___ rest for a week.`,explanation:`"Should" reports advice.`,options:[`can`,`shall`,`should`,`will`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Shall" is not used in reported advice.`,3:`"Will" is a definite future.`,0:`"Can" does not give advice.`}},{id:`c1-vf-354`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`We look forward ___ hearing from you at your earliest convenience.`,explanation:`"Look forward to" is followed by -ing.`,options:[`for`,`at`,`to`,`on`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Look forward for" is not standard.`,1:`"Look forward at" is not standard.`,3:`"Look forward on" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-vf-355`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`There is little that ___ can do to alter the outcome.`,explanation:`"Anyone" is used with a negative sense.`,options:[`someone`,`no one`,`everyone`,`anyone`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Little that everyone can do" changes the meaning.`,0:`"Someone" is not used in negatives.`,1:`"No one" would give a double negative.`}},{id:`c1-vf-356`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`I wish I ___ so impulsive when I was younger; I would have saved myself a lot of trouble.`,explanation:`"Wish" + past perfect expresses regret.`,options:[`haven't been`,`weren't`,`wasn't`,`hadn't been`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`A wish about the past needs the past perfect.`,1:`This refers to the present.`,0:`The present perfect does not fit.`}},{id:`c1-vf-357`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`You ___ have mentioned it before; now we have no time to prepare.`,explanation:`"Could have" expresses a missed chance.`,options:[`can`,`will`,`could`,`may to`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Can have mentioned" does not express a missed chance.`,1:`"Will have" does not fit.`,3:`"May to" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-vf-358`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`everyday-life`],prompt:`I wish I ___ more attention to my teachers when I was at school.`,explanation:`"Wish" + past perfect expresses regret.`,options:[`had paid`,`have paid`,`paid`,`would pay`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`A wish about the past needs the past perfect.`,3:`"Would" does not express past regret.`,1:`"Have paid" is not the past perfect.`}},{id:`c1-vf-359`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`I have never had ___ objection to your plan.`,explanation:`"Any" is used in negatives.`,options:[`a few`,`any`,`some`,`no`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{2:`"Some" is not used in negatives like this.`,3:`"Never ... no" is a double negative.`,0:`"Objection" is singular.`}},{id:`c1-vf-360`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The number of people living in poverty ___ risen sharply over the past decade.`,explanation:`"The number of" takes a singular verb.`,options:[`had`,`is`,`have`,`has`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"The number of" takes a singular verb.`,0:`The present perfect is needed.`,1:`"Is risen" is not a correct perfect.`}},{id:`c1-vf-361`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`You ___ well wonder why nobody noticed the error earlier.`,explanation:`"May well" is a fixed idiom.`,options:[`should`,`would`,`will`,`may`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`"Will well wonder" is not idiomatic.`,1:`"Would well wonder" is not idiomatic.`,0:`"Should well wonder" is not idiomatic.`}},{id:`c1-vf-362`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`The thieves are believed ___ the country by now.`,explanation:`The perfect infinitive shows an earlier time.`,options:[`leaving`,`to have left`,`having left`,`to leave`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`The perfect infinitive refers to an earlier time.`,0:`A participle does not follow "believed".`,2:`"Believed" takes an infinitive.`}},{id:`c1-vf-363`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`She is not accustomed ___ being contradicted.`,explanation:`"Accustomed to" is followed by -ing.`,options:[`with`,`for`,`to`,`at`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Accustomed for" is not standard.`,0:`"Accustomed with" is not standard.`,3:`"Accustomed at" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-vf-364`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`By the time we reach the airport, the check-in desk ___ closed.`,explanation:`"By the time" + future perfect.`,options:[`would have`,`is having`,`will have`,`has`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Would" does not fit a real future.`,3:`A present tense does not fit.`,1:`The continuous does not fit.`}},{id:`c1-vf-365`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`He ___ be reached at the office on weekdays, but never at weekends.`,explanation:`"Can be reached" states a possibility of contact.`,options:[`can`,`would to`,`had`,`ought`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`"Would to" is ungrammatical.`,3:`"Ought be reached" lacks "to".`,2:`"Had be reached" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-vf-366`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`I cannot think of anyone ___ would be better suited to the post.`,explanation:`"Who" is the subject.`,options:[`whom`,`who`,`whose`,`which`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`"Whom" is an object form.`,2:`"Whose" shows possession.`,3:`"Which" refers to things.`}},{id:`c1-vf-367`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`pronouns`,topics:[`pronouns-relative-clauses`],prompt:`There was nobody there ___ could help us.`,explanation:`"Who" is the subject of the relative clause.`,options:[`who`,`whom`,`which`,`whose`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{1:`The relative acts as the subject.`,3:`"Whose" shows possession.`,2:`"Which" refers to things.`}},{id:`c1-vf-368`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`The itinerary, ___ had been agreed months earlier, was changed at the last moment.`,explanation:`"Which" introduces a nonrestrictive clause.`,options:[`what`,`that`,`whose`,`which`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"What" cannot introduce a relative clause.`,1:`"That" cannot follow a comma.`,2:`"Whose" shows possession.`}},{id:`c1-vf-369`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`It's essential that every member ___ the rules.`,explanation:`"Essential that" takes the base form.`,options:[`follow`,`following`,`follows`,`will follow`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{2:`The mandative subjunctive uses the base form.`,3:`"Will" is not used here.`,1:`A finite form is needed.`}},{id:`c1-vf-370`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`They were united ___ their opposition to the plan.`,explanation:`"United in" introduces the shared feeling.`,options:[`by`,`in`,`at`,`on`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"United on" is not standard.`,2:`"United at" is not standard.`,0:`"United by" would introduce a cause of union.`}},{id:`c1-vf-371`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`tenses`,topics:[`verb-tenses`],prompt:`I ___ the report for hours and still have not finished.`,explanation:`The present perfect continuous links duration to now.`,options:[`am writing`,`write`,`wrote`,`have been writing`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`The present simple does not show duration.`,0:`The present continuous does not show duration.`,2:`The simple past does not link to now.`}},{id:`c1-vf-372`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`prepositions`,topics:[`prepositions`],prompt:`The team is capable ___ winning the championship.`,explanation:`"Capable of" is followed by -ing.`,options:[`to`,`for`,`of`,`at`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{0:`"Capable to" is not standard.`,1:`"Capable for" is not standard.`,3:`"Capable at" is not standard.`}},{id:`c1-vf-373`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`modals`,topics:[`modal-verbs`],prompt:`It is vital that the manager ___ informed immediately.`,explanation:`"Vital that" takes the base form.`,options:[`is`,`be`,`will be`,`being`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{0:`The mandative subjunctive uses the base form.`,2:`"Will" is not used here.`,3:`A finite verb is needed.`}},{id:`c1-vf-374`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`travel-places-leisure`],prompt:`Not only ___ the flight delayed, but our luggage also went missing.`,explanation:`"Not only" at the start requires inversion.`,options:[`did`,`were`,`has`,`was`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{1:`"The flight" is singular.`,0:`"Did the flight delayed" is ungrammatical.`,2:`"Has the flight delayed" is ungrammatical.`}},{id:`c1-vf-375`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`vocabulary`,topics:[`society-health-environment`],prompt:`The more research is done on the subject, the more complicated it ___.`,explanation:`The present simple states the trend.`,options:[`seems`,`seemed`,`seeming`,`seem`],correct:0,wrongReasons:{3:`"It" takes a singular verb.`,2:`An auxiliary is missing.`,1:`The sentence states a current trend.`}},{id:`c1-vf-376`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`The team made ___ progress in the second half of the season.`,explanation:`"Steady progress" is a standard collocation.`,options:[`steadied`,`steadiness`,`steadily`,`steady`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{2:`An adjective is needed.`,1:`"Steadiness" is a noun.`,0:`"Steadied" is a verb form.`}},{id:`c1-vf-377`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`passive`,topics:[`passive-reported-speech`],prompt:`He is thought ___ the country shortly before the scandal broke.`,explanation:`The perfect infinitive shows an earlier time.`,options:[`having left`,`to leave`,`to have left`,`leaving`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`The perfect infinitive refers to an earlier time.`,3:`A participle does not follow "thought".`,0:`"Thought" takes an infinitive.`}},{id:`c1-vf-378`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`___ information in the leaflet was out of date.`,explanation:`"The" refers to specific information.`,options:[`Some`,`An`,`The`,`A`],correct:2,wrongReasons:{1:`"Information" is uncountable.`,0:`"Some" does not refer to specific information.`,3:`"A" does not work here, because "Information" is uncountable.`}},{id:`c1-vf-379`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Hardly ___ of the audience stayed until the end.`,explanation:`"Hardly any of" means almost none.`,options:[`some`,`any`,`all`,`every`],correct:1,wrongReasons:{3:`"Hardly every" is ungrammatical.`,0:`"Hardly some" is ungrammatical.`,2:`"Hardly all" has another sense.`}},{id:`c1-vf-380`,type:`verb-form`,level:`C1`,topic:`articles`,topics:[`articles-determiners`],prompt:`Not ___ of the committee members supported the amendment.`,explanation:`"Not all of" is a partial negation.`,options:[`every`,`each`,`whole`,`all`],correct:3,wrongReasons:{0:`"Every of" is ungrammatical.`,2:`"Whole of the" has another structure.`,1:`"Not each of" is possible but changes the sense.`}}]};export{e as default};